1
00:00:02,249 --> 00:00:02,353
you

2
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:03,566
What's going on here?

3
00:03:39,670 --> 00:03:41,752
for the powder room, and that's the last I see you.

4
00:03:42,052 --> 00:03:43,133
Now, really, Harry?

5
00:03:43,193 --> 00:03:44,514
Harry was the other guy.

6
00:03:44,534 --> 00:03:45,414
I'm Sid.

7
00:03:45,615 --> 00:03:46,295
Sid Arbuck.

8
00:03:46,535 --> 00:03:47,756
You like me, remember?

9
00:03:48,016 --> 00:03:49,157
Mr. Gold Rifery!

10
00:03:49,938 --> 00:03:51,519
I protest!

11
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:54,942
Oh, darling, I am sorry, but I lost my key.

12
00:03:55,342 --> 00:03:57,063
But that was two weeks ago.

13
00:03:57,284 --> 00:03:59,826
You cannot go on or keep ringing my bell.

14
00:04:00,146 --> 00:04:01,287
You disturb me.

15
00:04:01,487 --> 00:04:03,128
You must have a key made.

16
00:04:03,648 --> 00:04:04,489
But it won't do any good.

17
00:04:04,529 --> 00:04:05,410
I just lose them all.

18
00:04:05,930 --> 00:04:06,690
Come on, baby.

19
00:04:06,810 --> 00:04:07,551
You like me.

20
00:04:07,571 --> 00:04:08,291
You know you do.

21
00:04:08,411 --> 00:04:10,852
I worship you, Mr. Auerbach, but good night, Mr. Auerbach.

22
00:04:10,872 --> 00:04:11,392
Wait a minute.

23
00:04:11,412 --> 00:04:12,033
What is this?

24
00:04:12,433 --> 00:04:13,033
You like me.

25
00:04:13,093 --> 00:04:14,034
I'm a liked guy.

26
00:04:14,834 --> 00:04:15,714
You like me, baby.

27
00:04:15,734 --> 00:04:16,315
You know you do.

28
00:04:16,535 --> 00:04:18,215
Didn't I pick up the check for five people?

29
00:04:18,355 --> 00:04:18,956
Your friends?

30
00:04:18,996 --> 00:04:19,936
I never seen them before.

31
00:04:20,156 --> 00:04:22,857
And when you ask for a little change for the powder room, what do I give you?

32
00:04:23,058 --> 00:04:23,978
A $50 bill.

33
00:04:24,158 --> 00:04:26,839
Now, doesn't that give me some rights?

34
00:04:26,979 --> 00:04:29,160
In 30 seconds, I got to call the police.

35
00:04:30,261 --> 00:04:31,781
All the time a disturbance.

36
00:04:32,202 --> 00:04:33,122
I can't no sleep.

37
00:04:33,662 --> 00:04:34,642
I got to get to my rest.

38
00:04:34,762 --> 00:04:35,563
I'm an artist.

39
00:04:36,383 --> 00:04:38,684
Or I got to call the vice squad on you.

40
00:04:39,164 --> 00:04:40,485
Don't be angry, you dear little man.

41
00:04:40,505 --> 00:04:41,265
I won't do it again.

42
00:04:41,285 --> 00:04:44,546
If you promise not to be angry, I might let you take those pictures we mentioned.

43
00:04:46,747 --> 00:04:47,227
When?

44
00:04:48,928 --> 00:04:49,388
Sometime.

45
00:04:52,069 --> 00:04:52,869
Anytime.

46
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:54,470
Good night.

47
00:06:20,658 --> 00:06:23,620
Sorry to bother you, but I couldn't get the downstairs door open.

48
00:06:26,421 --> 00:06:28,803
I guess they sent me the upstairs key.

49
00:06:28,863 --> 00:06:30,884
I couldn't get the downstairs door open.

50
00:06:33,746 --> 00:06:38,168
I said, I guess they sent me the upstairs key.

51
00:06:38,228 --> 00:06:39,909
I couldn't get the downstairs door open.

52
00:06:39,969 --> 00:06:41,510
I'm sorry to wake you.

53
00:06:41,990 --> 00:06:42,891
It's quite all right.

54
00:06:44,492 --> 00:06:47,153
Could happen to anyone, quite frequently does, couldn't it?

55
00:06:47,673 --> 00:06:48,274
I hate to...

56
00:06:50,530 --> 00:06:54,672
I hate to bother you, but if I could ask one more favor, could I use the phone?

57
00:06:57,353 --> 00:06:57,753
Sure.

58
00:06:59,474 --> 00:07:00,994
Why not?

59
00:07:01,054 --> 00:07:01,995
Thank you.

60
00:07:04,016 --> 00:07:07,737
Well, this is a nice little place you've got here.

61
00:07:09,278 --> 00:07:10,318
You just moved in too, huh?

62
00:07:10,878 --> 00:07:11,138
No.

63
00:07:11,439 --> 00:07:12,499
I've been here about a year.

64
00:07:13,119 --> 00:07:14,240
The phone's over there.

65
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:16,861
Well, it was.

66
00:07:16,881 --> 00:07:19,762
Oh, I remember.

67
00:07:20,662 --> 00:07:21,802
I stuck it in the suitcase.

68
00:07:21,822 --> 00:07:23,163
Kind of muffles the sound.

69
00:07:31,486 --> 00:07:32,627
I'm sorry.

70
00:07:34,147 --> 00:07:34,788
Is he all right?

71
00:07:35,228 --> 00:07:35,808
Sure.

72
00:07:36,488 --> 00:07:38,229
Sure he's okay, aren't you, cat?

73
00:07:38,969 --> 00:07:40,390
Poor old cat.

74
00:07:41,190 --> 00:07:42,491
Poor slob.

75
00:07:42,931 --> 00:07:44,392
Poor slob without a name.

76
00:07:44,412 --> 00:07:47,593
The way I look at it, I don't have the right to give him one.

77
00:07:47,633 --> 00:07:48,733
We don't belong to each other.

78
00:07:48,753 --> 00:07:50,274
We just took up by the river one day.

79
00:07:51,274 --> 00:07:54,915
I don't even want to own anything until I can find a place where me and things go together.

80
00:07:55,955 --> 00:07:59,376
I'm not sure where that is, but I know what it's like.

81
00:08:01,077 --> 00:08:02,017
It's like Tiffany's.

82
00:08:02,717 --> 00:08:03,217
Tiffany's?

83
00:08:03,898 --> 00:08:04,878
You mean the jewelry store?

84
00:08:05,338 --> 00:08:05,878
That's right.

85
00:08:07,179 --> 00:08:09,099
I'm crazy about Tiffany's.

86
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:11,040
Listen.

87
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:14,901
You know those days when you get the mean reds?

88
00:08:15,881 --> 00:08:16,461
The mean reds?

89
00:08:16,921 --> 00:08:17,682
You mean like the blues?

90
00:08:18,562 --> 00:08:18,782
No.

91
00:08:19,714 --> 00:08:22,517
The blues are because you're getting fat or maybe it's been raining too long.

92
00:08:22,617 --> 00:08:23,858
You're just sad, that's all.

93
00:08:25,359 --> 00:08:26,560
The mean reds are horrible.

94
00:08:27,121 --> 00:08:29,203
Suddenly you're afraid and you don't know what you're afraid of.

95
00:08:30,764 --> 00:08:31,985
Did you ever get that feeling?

96
00:08:33,066 --> 00:08:34,407
Sure.

97
00:08:34,427 --> 00:08:38,952
When I get it, the only thing that does any good is to jump into a cab and go to Tiffany's.

98
00:08:39,432 --> 00:08:40,893
It calms me down right away.

99
00:08:42,214 --> 00:08:43,896
The quietness and the proud look of it.

100
00:08:44,236 --> 00:08:45,938
Nothing very bad could happen to you there.

101
00:08:46,850 --> 00:08:55,057
If I could find a real life place that made me feel like Tiffany's, then I'd buy some furniture and give the cat a name.

102
00:08:58,179 --> 00:08:59,481
I'm sorry you wanted something.

103
00:09:00,241 --> 00:09:01,582
Oh, the telephone.

104
00:09:02,563 --> 00:09:04,164
It's just that I was supposed to meet somebody.

105
00:09:04,905 --> 00:09:07,667
I mean, this is 10 o'clock Thursday morning, isn't it?

106
00:09:08,168 --> 00:09:10,250
I just got off a plane from Rome, and I'm not too sure.

107
00:09:11,110 --> 00:09:11,751
Thursday?

108
00:09:12,912 --> 00:09:13,812
Is this Thursday?

109
00:09:14,033 --> 00:09:14,513
I think so.

110
00:09:14,593 --> 00:09:15,194
Thursday?

111
00:09:16,482 --> 00:09:17,542
It's too gruesome.

112
00:09:21,564 --> 00:09:24,384
Well, what's so gruesome about Thursday?

113
00:09:24,724 --> 00:09:26,925
Nothing except I can never remember when it's coming up.

114
00:09:27,885 --> 00:09:31,586
Wednesdays I generally just don't go to bed at all because I have to be up to catch the 10.45.

115
00:09:32,126 --> 00:09:34,367
And they're so particular about visiting hours.

116
00:09:35,307 --> 00:09:38,708
Would you be a darling to look under the bed and see if you can find a pair of alligator shoes?

117
00:09:39,409 --> 00:09:39,709
Sure.

118
00:09:43,010 --> 00:09:44,530
I've got to do something about the way I look.

119
00:09:45,130 --> 00:09:47,191
I mean, a girl just can't go to Sing Sing with a green face.

120
00:09:48,291 --> 00:09:48,832
Sing Sing?

121
00:09:49,212 --> 00:09:53,394
Yes, I always thought it was a ridiculous name for a prison.

122
00:09:53,414 --> 00:09:54,234
Sing Sing, I mean.

123
00:09:54,754 --> 00:09:56,575
Sounds more like it should be an opera house or something.

124
00:09:57,495 --> 00:09:58,416
Black Alligator.

125
00:09:59,836 --> 00:10:02,017
You know, all the visitors make an effort to look their best.

126
00:10:02,297 --> 00:10:02,998
It's only fair.

127
00:10:03,698 --> 00:10:06,099
Actually, it's very touching, all the women wearing their prettiest things.

128
00:10:06,139 --> 00:10:07,120
I just love them for it.

129
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:08,920
And I love the kids, too.

130
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:10,161
I mean, the kids the wives bring.

131
00:10:10,821 --> 00:10:12,682
It should be sad seeing kids there, but it isn't.

132
00:10:13,314 --> 00:10:16,115
They all have ribbons in their hair and lots of shine on their shoes.

133
00:10:16,695 --> 00:10:18,176
You'd think there was going to be ice cream.

134
00:10:18,796 --> 00:10:22,498
Now, as I understand it, what we're doing is getting you ready to visit somebody at Sing Sing.

135
00:10:22,738 --> 00:10:23,198
That's right.

136
00:10:24,118 --> 00:10:27,600
You can always tell what kind of a person a man really thinks you are by the earrings he gives you.

137
00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:30,081
I must say, the mind reels.

138
00:10:30,881 --> 00:10:31,782
May I ask whom?

139
00:10:32,122 --> 00:10:32,542
Whom what?

140
00:10:33,222 --> 00:10:34,283
Oh, whom I go to visit you mean.

141
00:10:34,823 --> 00:10:35,843
I guess that's what I mean.

142
00:10:36,263 --> 00:10:37,624
I don't know that I should even discuss it.

143
00:10:38,404 --> 00:10:40,005
Oh, well, they never told me not to tell anyone.

144
00:10:40,725 --> 00:10:42,166
You must cross your heart and kiss your elbow.

145
00:10:43,614 --> 00:10:44,054
I'll try.

146
00:10:45,155 --> 00:10:46,015
You've probably read about him.

147
00:10:46,035 --> 00:10:47,136
His name's Sally Tomato.

148
00:10:48,176 --> 00:10:49,016
Sally Tomato?

149
00:10:49,577 --> 00:10:50,457
Oh, don't look so shocked.

150
00:10:50,957 --> 00:10:54,379
They could never prove for a second that he was even part of the mafia, much less head of it, my dear.

151
00:10:54,739 --> 00:10:57,600
The only thing they did prove was that he cheated at his income tax a little.

152
00:10:57,640 --> 00:10:59,641
Anyway, all I know is that he's a darling old man.

153
00:11:00,341 --> 00:11:02,122
Oh, he was never my lover or anything like that.

154
00:11:02,802 --> 00:11:04,723
In fact, I never knew him until after he was in prison.

155
00:11:04,963 --> 00:11:05,923
But I adore him now.

156
00:11:06,163 --> 00:11:08,524
I mean, I've been going to see him every Thursday for seven months.

157
00:11:08,985 --> 00:11:10,565
Now I think I'd go even if he didn't pay me.

158
00:11:11,546 --> 00:11:11,866
Shoes!

159
00:11:12,450 --> 00:11:13,370
You can only find one.

160
00:11:17,812 --> 00:11:18,552
He pays you?

161
00:11:19,332 --> 00:11:19,792
That's right.

162
00:11:20,112 --> 00:11:21,273
Or anyway, his lawyer does.

163
00:11:21,833 --> 00:11:25,314
If he is a lawyer, which I doubt since he doesn't seem to have an office, only an answering service.

164
00:11:25,634 --> 00:11:27,314
And he always wants to meet you at Hamburger Heaven.

165
00:11:30,835 --> 00:11:32,056
There you are, you sneak.

166
00:11:32,756 --> 00:11:33,096
Thank you.

167
00:11:33,116 --> 00:11:33,816
You're welcome.

168
00:11:34,597 --> 00:11:34,957
Dress.

169
00:11:35,777 --> 00:11:36,177
Dress.

170
00:11:37,257 --> 00:11:37,557
Here we are.

171
00:11:37,917 --> 00:11:38,378
Bag.

172
00:11:38,738 --> 00:11:39,098
There.

173
00:11:39,218 --> 00:11:39,458
Two.

174
00:11:41,366 --> 00:11:42,066
There we are.

175
00:11:42,466 --> 00:11:50,290
Anyway, about seven months ago, this so-called lawyer, Mr. O'Shaughnessy, asked me how I'd like to cheer up a lonely old man and pick up a hundred a week at the same time.

176
00:11:50,990 --> 00:11:54,131
I told him, look, darling, you've got the wrong Holly Golightly.

177
00:11:54,572 --> 00:11:56,633
A girl can do as well as level trips to the powder room.

178
00:11:57,293 --> 00:12:01,074
I mean, any gentleman with the slightest cheek will give a girl a $50 bill for the powder room.

179
00:12:01,415 --> 00:12:02,735
And I always ask for cab fare, too.

180
00:12:02,755 --> 00:12:03,596
That's another 50.

181
00:12:04,136 --> 00:12:06,157
But then he told me his client was Sally Tomato.

182
00:12:06,657 --> 00:12:09,278
He said, dear old Sally had seen me at Elmer's or somewhere.

183
00:12:09,642 --> 00:12:11,383
and had admired me a la distance.

184
00:12:11,783 --> 00:12:14,005
So wouldn't it be a good deed if I went to visit him once a week?

185
00:12:14,585 --> 00:12:15,645
Well, how could I say no?

186
00:12:15,665 --> 00:12:17,366
It was all so wildly romantic.

187
00:12:19,788 --> 00:12:20,368
How do I look?

188
00:12:22,710 --> 00:12:23,230
Very good.

189
00:12:24,130 --> 00:12:25,251
I must say I'm amazed.

190
00:12:25,731 --> 00:12:26,772
You were darling to help.

191
00:12:26,792 --> 00:12:27,953
I could never have done it without you.

192
00:12:28,953 --> 00:12:29,473
Back.

193
00:12:30,374 --> 00:12:31,154
Call me any time.

194
00:12:33,096 --> 00:12:33,936
Just upstairs.

195
00:12:34,556 --> 00:12:36,357
Or I will be as soon as I get moved in.

196
00:12:36,377 --> 00:12:37,218
Bye, Kat.

197
00:12:44,450 --> 00:12:47,672
You mean he gives you $100 for an hour's conversation?

198
00:12:47,692 --> 00:12:51,193
Well, Mr. O'Shaughnessy does as soon as I meet him and give him the weather report.

199
00:12:52,694 --> 00:12:55,815
Look, it's none of my business, but it sounds to me like you could get in a lot of trouble.

200
00:12:56,356 --> 00:12:57,276
Hold this for me, will you?

201
00:13:00,978 --> 00:13:02,818
And what do you mean, weather report?

202
00:13:03,699 --> 00:13:07,321
Oh, there's just a message I give Mr. O'Shaughnessy so he'll know I've really been up there.

203
00:13:08,541 --> 00:13:10,342
Sally tells me things to say like, uh...

204
00:13:11,022 --> 00:13:14,624
Oh, there's a hurricane in Cuba and it's cloudy over Palermo.

205
00:13:15,044 --> 00:13:15,785
Things like that.

206
00:13:16,385 --> 00:13:17,186
You don't have to worry.

207
00:13:17,206 --> 00:13:19,087
I've taken care of myself for a long time.

208
00:13:28,012 --> 00:13:28,472
Taxi!

209
00:13:36,917 --> 00:13:38,017
I never could do that.

210
00:13:38,458 --> 00:13:38,918
It's easy.

211
00:13:50,114 --> 00:13:50,594
I'm late.

212
00:13:51,275 --> 00:13:51,775
I know it.

213
00:13:54,798 --> 00:13:56,099
Don't tell me you were locked out.

214
00:13:56,239 --> 00:13:57,120
Didn't you get the key?

215
00:13:57,680 --> 00:13:58,381
Oh, darling.

216
00:13:59,322 --> 00:14:00,322
I'm so sorry.

217
00:14:02,564 --> 00:14:03,585
No, I got the key all right.

218
00:14:04,286 --> 00:14:06,928
Miss Golightly, my neighbor, was kind enough to let me in.

219
00:14:08,609 --> 00:14:09,830
Miss Golightly's on her way to Sing Sing.

220
00:14:10,511 --> 00:14:10,611
Oh.

221
00:14:10,931 --> 00:14:11,732
Just visiting, of course.

222
00:14:12,653 --> 00:14:15,995
Miss Golightly, Mrs. Phelanson, my decorator.

223
00:14:16,696 --> 00:14:17,257
How do you do?

224
00:14:18,277 --> 00:14:18,858
How do you do?

225
00:14:19,882 --> 00:14:21,282
Ooh, darling.

226
00:14:22,023 --> 00:14:23,263
Let me look at you.

227
00:14:24,443 --> 00:14:25,224
Are you through?

228
00:14:25,444 --> 00:14:27,444
I'm in a terrible rush.

229
00:14:28,524 --> 00:14:30,585
Grand Central Station and step on it, darling.

230
00:14:32,806 --> 00:14:36,126
Is it really only three weeks since I left you in Rome?

231
00:14:36,347 --> 00:14:37,347
Seems like years.

232
00:14:38,267 --> 00:14:39,187
You seen the apartment?

233
00:14:39,567 --> 00:14:39,968
Not yet.

234
00:14:41,448 --> 00:14:43,528
I know it was wicked of me, but I couldn't resist.

235
00:14:43,969 --> 00:14:45,669
I went ahead and fixed it up without you.

236
00:14:46,169 --> 00:14:48,970
I think it's darling, of course, but if you absolutely hate it,

237
00:14:49,290 --> 00:15:03,100
We can rip everything out and start from scratch.

238
00:15:03,460 --> 00:15:04,761
Over the car.

239
00:15:04,821 --> 00:15:08,223
Hey, baby!

240
00:15:08,624 --> 00:15:09,424
Where you going?

241
00:15:12,166 --> 00:15:13,207
Oh, come on, baby.

242
00:15:13,267 --> 00:15:14,628
Open the door.

243
00:15:16,269 --> 00:15:17,750
Oh, be a pal.

244
00:15:18,786 --> 00:15:20,987
You're breaking up a beautiful party.

245
00:15:21,467 --> 00:15:25,588
Come on, baby, open the door.

246
00:15:26,328 --> 00:15:28,069
Hey, the band is swinging.

247
00:15:29,729 --> 00:15:31,529
Oh, come on, baby.

248
00:15:33,470 --> 00:15:37,391
Mr. Golightly, once again, I must protest.

249
00:15:37,971 --> 00:15:42,753
If you don't stop with that phonograph right this minute, I'm going to call the police department.

250
00:15:45,493 --> 00:15:47,814
Well, that's more better.

251
00:15:52,246 --> 00:15:56,951
What's the matter, baby?

252
00:15:58,753 --> 00:15:59,674
Oh, come on.

253
00:15:59,714 --> 00:16:00,755
You're a great kid.

254
00:16:00,795 --> 00:16:01,936
Open the door.

255
00:16:04,899 --> 00:16:05,960
Come on, baby.

256
00:16:06,060 --> 00:16:07,321
I'm waiting for you.

257
00:17:07,394 --> 00:17:07,714
It's all right.

258
00:17:07,734 --> 00:17:08,254
It's only me.

259
00:17:09,315 --> 00:17:11,516
Uh, no, wait a minute, Miss, uh... Golightly.

260
00:17:12,037 --> 00:17:12,777
Holly Golightly.

261
00:17:13,918 --> 00:17:14,598
I live downstairs.

262
00:17:14,618 --> 00:17:15,679
We met this morning, remember?

263
00:17:16,459 --> 00:17:16,759
Yeah.

264
00:17:19,201 --> 00:17:19,601
It's all right.

265
00:17:19,621 --> 00:17:20,081
She's gone.

266
00:17:20,101 --> 00:17:24,143
I must say, she works late hours for a decorator.

267
00:17:25,584 --> 00:17:27,545
The thing is, I have the most terrifying man downstairs.

268
00:17:28,046 --> 00:17:32,788
I mean, he's sweet when he isn't drunk, but let him start lapping up the vino, and, oh, golly, Kel Beast.

269
00:17:33,529 --> 00:17:35,590
Finally got so tiresome down there, just went out the window.

270
00:17:42,210 --> 00:17:49,932
You can throw me out if you want to, but you did look so cozy in here, and your decorative friend had gone home, and it was beginning to get a bit cold out there on the fire escape.

271
00:17:51,673 --> 00:17:55,354
And I always heard people in New York never get to know their neighbors.

272
00:17:57,714 --> 00:18:00,795
Well, how was Sing Sing?

273
00:18:01,535 --> 00:18:01,835
Fine.

274
00:18:03,976 --> 00:18:05,036
I made the tray and everything.

275
00:18:05,976 --> 00:18:07,017
And what's the weather report?

276
00:18:08,677 --> 00:18:11,378
Small craft warnings, Block Island, Hatteras.

277
00:18:12,118 --> 00:18:12,898
Whatever that means.

278
00:18:16,700 --> 00:18:17,441
You know you're sweet?

279
00:18:18,281 --> 00:18:18,942
You really are.

280
00:18:21,423 --> 00:18:22,864
And you look a little like my brother Fred.

281
00:18:22,884 --> 00:18:25,645
Do you mind if I call you Fred?

282
00:18:26,946 --> 00:18:33,169
Not at all.

283
00:18:33,209 --> 00:18:33,469
300.

284
00:18:33,490 --> 00:18:34,250
She's very generous.

285
00:18:34,270 --> 00:18:38,852
Is it by the week, the hour, or what?

286
00:18:38,952 --> 00:18:39,853
OK, the party's over.

287
00:18:39,933 --> 00:18:40,133
Out.

288
00:18:40,513 --> 00:18:41,274
Poor Fred.

289
00:18:41,954 --> 00:18:43,235
Darling Fred, I'm sorry.

290
00:18:43,255 --> 00:18:45,056
I didn't mean to hurt your feelings.

291
00:18:45,076 --> 00:18:45,876
Don't be angry.

292
00:18:45,896 --> 00:18:49,538
I was just trying to let you know I understand.

293
00:18:50,439 --> 00:18:51,439
I understand completely.

294
00:18:51,459 --> 00:18:53,841
It's okay.

295
00:18:53,861 --> 00:18:54,241
Stick around.

296
00:18:54,261 --> 00:18:55,001
Make yourself a drink.

297
00:18:55,842 --> 00:18:57,063
Throw me my robe and I'll make you one.

298
00:18:57,123 --> 00:18:58,143
You stay right where you are.

299
00:18:58,443 --> 00:18:59,944
You must be absolutely exhausted.

300
00:19:01,405 --> 00:19:04,447
I mean, it is very late and you were sound asleep and everything.

301
00:19:04,467 --> 00:19:09,730
I suppose you think I'm very brazen or très fou or something.

302
00:19:10,482 --> 00:19:12,283
I don't think you're any fuller than anybody else.

303
00:19:12,504 --> 00:19:12,964
Yes, you do.

304
00:19:13,004 --> 00:19:14,325
Everybody does, and I don't mind.

305
00:19:15,466 --> 00:19:17,787
It's useful being top banana in the shock department.

306
00:19:22,071 --> 00:19:22,991
What do you do, anyway?

307
00:19:23,892 --> 00:19:24,712
I'm a writer, I guess.

308
00:19:26,394 --> 00:19:27,314
You guess, don't you know?

309
00:19:27,334 --> 00:19:28,916
Okay.

310
00:19:29,356 --> 00:19:30,257
Positive statement.

311
00:19:30,337 --> 00:19:31,397
Ringing affirmative.

312
00:19:32,498 --> 00:19:33,019
I'm a writer.

313
00:19:35,260 --> 00:19:37,522
The only writer I would have been out with is Benny Shacklett.

314
00:19:38,846 --> 00:19:41,728
He's written an awful lot of television stuff, but Kel Rath.

315
00:19:43,429 --> 00:19:45,270
Tell me, are you a real writer?

316
00:19:46,070 --> 00:19:49,472
I mean, does anybody buy what you write or publish it or anything?

317
00:19:50,753 --> 00:19:51,913
They bought what's in the box.

318
00:19:54,575 --> 00:19:55,055
Yours?

319
00:19:55,575 --> 00:19:55,935
Mm-hmm.

320
00:19:57,116 --> 00:19:57,816
All these books?

321
00:19:58,557 --> 00:19:59,878
Oh, it's just the one book.

322
00:20:00,358 --> 00:20:01,278
Twelve copies of it.

323
00:20:01,959 --> 00:20:04,020
Nine Lives by Paul Barjack.

324
00:20:05,301 --> 00:20:05,941
They're stories.

325
00:20:06,661 --> 00:20:06,981
Mm-hmm.

326
00:20:07,522 --> 00:20:07,862
Nine of them.

327
00:20:10,422 --> 00:20:10,942
Tell me more.

328
00:20:13,024 --> 00:20:15,045
They're not the kind of stories you can really tell.

329
00:20:15,666 --> 00:20:16,186
Too dirty?

330
00:20:18,968 --> 00:20:21,990
I suppose they're dirty too, but only incidentally.

331
00:20:22,771 --> 00:20:30,436
Mainly they're angry, sensitive, intensely felt, and the dirtiest of all dirty words, promising.

332
00:20:31,897 --> 00:20:34,179
Or so said the Times Book Review, October 1st, 1956.

333
00:20:34,239 --> 00:20:34,379
1956?

334
00:20:34,419 --> 00:20:34,919
That's right.

335
00:20:39,874 --> 00:20:43,155
I suppose this is kind of a ratty question, but what have you written lately?

336
00:20:44,816 --> 00:20:46,037
Lately, I've been working on a novel.

337
00:20:47,517 --> 00:20:47,857
Lately?

338
00:20:47,877 --> 00:20:48,358
Since 1956?

339
00:20:48,378 --> 00:20:50,598
Well, a novel takes a long time.

340
00:20:50,618 --> 00:20:51,699
I want to get it exactly right.

341
00:20:53,180 --> 00:20:54,060
So no more stories?

342
00:20:54,780 --> 00:20:59,622
Well, the idea is I'm supposed to not fritter my talent away on little things.

343
00:20:59,682 --> 00:21:01,363
I'm supposed to be saving it for the big one.

344
00:21:03,144 --> 00:21:04,384
Tell me, do you write every day?

345
00:21:05,285 --> 00:21:05,525
Sure.

346
00:21:07,125 --> 00:21:07,466
Today?

347
00:21:08,226 --> 00:21:08,446
Sure.

348
00:21:09,730 --> 00:21:10,751
It's a beautiful typewriter.

349
00:21:11,331 --> 00:21:11,771
Of course.

350
00:21:12,071 --> 00:21:15,173
It writes nothing but sensitive, intensely-felt, promising prose.

351
00:21:15,613 --> 00:21:16,514
But there's no ribbon in it.

352
00:21:18,155 --> 00:21:18,515
There isn't?

353
00:21:19,676 --> 00:21:19,876
No.

354
00:21:22,017 --> 00:21:22,157
Oh.

355
00:21:26,419 --> 00:21:28,761
You know, something you said this morning's been bothering me all day.

356
00:21:29,881 --> 00:21:30,302
What's that?

357
00:21:30,322 --> 00:21:34,204
Do they really give you $50 whenever you go to the powder room?

358
00:21:34,864 --> 00:21:35,344
Of course.

359
00:21:37,145 --> 00:21:38,046
You must do very well.

360
00:21:39,426 --> 00:21:41,187
I'm trying to save, but I'm not very good at it.

361
00:21:48,412 --> 00:21:50,153
You know, you do look a lot like my brother Fred.

362
00:21:52,074 --> 00:21:53,836
I haven't seen him, of course, since I was 14.

363
00:21:53,856 --> 00:21:54,716
That's when I left home.

364
00:21:54,736 --> 00:21:57,918
And he was already six foot two.

365
00:21:57,938 --> 00:22:01,561
I guess it must have been the peanut butter that did it.

366
00:22:03,062 --> 00:22:06,084
Everybody thought he was Dottie, the way he gorged himself on peanut butter.

367
00:22:08,165 --> 00:22:09,066
But he wasn't Dottie.

368
00:22:10,658 --> 00:22:13,840
Just sweet and vague and terribly slow.

369
00:22:16,542 --> 00:22:17,363
Poor Fred.

370
00:22:18,844 --> 00:22:19,685
He's in the army now.

371
00:22:19,705 --> 00:22:23,067
That's really the best place for him until I can get enough money saved.

372
00:22:24,648 --> 00:22:25,009
And then?

373
00:22:26,250 --> 00:22:27,230
And then Fred and I...

374
00:22:29,372 --> 00:22:30,353
I went to Mexico once.

375
00:22:31,574 --> 00:22:33,155
It's a wonderful place for raising horses.

376
00:22:35,697 --> 00:22:37,398
I saw one place near the sea that...

377
00:22:39,350 --> 00:22:40,530
Fred's very good with horses.

378
00:22:43,031 --> 00:22:44,772
But even land in Mexico costs something.

379
00:22:45,352 --> 00:22:48,673
And no matter what I do, there never seems to be more than a couple of hundred dollars in the bank.

380
00:22:51,673 --> 00:22:52,634
It can't be 4.30.

381
00:22:52,814 --> 00:22:55,855
It just can't.

382
00:22:59,936 --> 00:23:03,717
Do you mind if I just get in with you for a minute?

383
00:23:06,558 --> 00:23:07,138
It's all right.

384
00:23:07,518 --> 00:23:08,018
Really, it is.

385
00:23:10,506 --> 00:23:11,667
We're friends, that's all.

386
00:23:16,289 --> 00:23:17,290
We are friends, aren't we?

387
00:23:19,631 --> 00:23:25,254
Sure.

388
00:23:28,376 --> 00:23:31,778
OK. Let's don't say another word.

389
00:24:28,064 --> 00:24:29,731
Let's just get one thing straight right now.

390
00:24:32,201 --> 00:24:33,145
I hate snoops.

391
00:25:25,710 --> 00:25:26,570
Lucille, darling?

392
00:25:27,171 --> 00:25:28,311
Two E. Huh?

393
00:25:29,331 --> 00:25:31,292
I've been trying desperately to reach you.

394
00:25:31,952 --> 00:25:32,952
Bill just got back.

395
00:25:33,552 --> 00:25:34,713
A day early, the beast.

396
00:25:35,413 --> 00:25:36,913
So I'm afraid I'll have to beg off.

397
00:25:37,633 --> 00:25:39,194
You'll explain to the rest of the girls?

398
00:25:41,534 --> 00:25:42,395
You're a darling.

399
00:25:43,295 --> 00:25:45,335
Maybe we can have a long lunch tomorrow.

400
00:25:46,596 --> 00:25:47,736
I'll phone you in the morning.

401
00:25:48,116 --> 00:25:48,396
Mm-hmm.

402
00:25:49,477 --> 00:25:50,097
Whatever you say.

403
00:25:51,157 --> 00:25:53,478
And you will manage to survive without me tonight.

404
00:25:54,358 --> 00:25:54,678
Sure.

405
00:25:56,018 --> 00:25:58,827
I might even take a wild, boyish fling at writing.

406
00:26:00,212 --> 00:26:00,773
Good night.

407
00:26:01,576 --> 00:26:01,917
Good night.

408
00:26:28,538 --> 00:26:29,458
Huh, Polly baby?

409
00:26:30,879 --> 00:26:31,739
Since you're my big mouth.

410
00:26:37,701 --> 00:26:38,401
Buongiorno.

411
00:26:39,581 --> 00:26:39,821
Huh?

412
00:26:41,121 --> 00:26:41,842
Aren't you drinking?

413
00:26:43,382 --> 00:26:43,762
Mm-hmm.

414
00:26:44,182 --> 00:26:46,463
Oh, you got pockets there or something?

415
00:26:47,543 --> 00:26:48,203
What do you go by?

416
00:26:49,484 --> 00:26:49,984
What's your name?

417
00:26:50,064 --> 00:26:51,064
What do you call yourself?

418
00:26:52,825 --> 00:26:53,145
Irving.

419
00:26:54,645 --> 00:26:56,186
Oh, yeah, perfect, perfect.

420
00:26:56,326 --> 00:26:57,366
That's wonderful, Irving.

421
00:26:59,810 --> 00:27:00,830
I'll be right back, Irving, baby.

422
00:27:09,052 --> 00:27:09,413
Yeah.

423
00:27:09,633 --> 00:27:10,813
Oh, kid's still in the shower.

424
00:27:10,833 --> 00:27:11,293
You expected?

425
00:27:11,633 --> 00:27:12,173
I was invited.

426
00:27:12,193 --> 00:27:13,014
Is that what you mean?

427
00:27:13,294 --> 00:27:15,614
Oh, now, don't get yourself all tense and sore, pal.

428
00:27:15,634 --> 00:27:16,655
Come on in.

429
00:27:16,715 --> 00:27:17,235
It's a party.

430
00:27:17,875 --> 00:27:19,095
There's a lot of characters coming around here.

431
00:27:19,115 --> 00:27:19,775
They're not expected.

432
00:27:20,376 --> 00:27:20,936
I'll buy you a drink.

433
00:27:20,996 --> 00:27:21,336
You drink?

434
00:27:21,576 --> 00:27:21,816
Yeah.

435
00:27:22,056 --> 00:27:22,576
I'll buy you a drink.

436
00:27:22,816 --> 00:27:24,117
OK. Yeah.

437
00:27:24,557 --> 00:27:26,617
Hey, honey, you're a stead split, man.

438
00:27:27,437 --> 00:27:28,798
What do you drink, kid?

439
00:27:28,998 --> 00:27:29,318
Bourbon.

440
00:27:29,694 --> 00:27:29,934
All right.

441
00:27:30,494 --> 00:27:30,974
On the rocks?

442
00:27:31,254 --> 00:27:31,655
Yeah.

443
00:27:31,675 --> 00:27:31,895
Good.

444
00:27:31,935 --> 00:27:32,695
No, with water.

445
00:27:33,055 --> 00:27:33,275
All right.

446
00:27:33,295 --> 00:27:34,355
You mind rocks first, then, won't you?

447
00:27:34,675 --> 00:27:35,136
Uh, yeah.

448
00:27:35,256 --> 00:27:35,396
Good.

449
00:27:35,416 --> 00:27:35,736
You got it.

450
00:27:36,276 --> 00:27:36,736
Not too much.

451
00:27:36,956 --> 00:27:37,356
All right.

452
00:27:37,756 --> 00:27:38,817
Yeah, that'll set you free.

453
00:27:38,937 --> 00:27:40,497
You not a kid long?

454
00:27:40,877 --> 00:27:41,357
Not very.

455
00:27:41,417 --> 00:27:42,057
I live upstairs.

456
00:27:42,238 --> 00:27:42,698
You're kidding.

457
00:27:43,278 --> 00:27:44,458
Oh, look at this place, will you?

458
00:27:44,498 --> 00:27:45,238
What a place.

459
00:27:45,298 --> 00:27:46,079
It's unbelievable.

460
00:27:46,099 --> 00:27:46,659
What a thump.

461
00:27:46,679 --> 00:27:46,759
Hmm?

462
00:27:46,899 --> 00:27:47,119
Huh.

463
00:27:47,279 --> 00:27:48,159
What do you think?

464
00:27:48,759 --> 00:27:49,159
About what?

465
00:27:50,260 --> 00:27:51,360
Is she or isn't she?

466
00:27:52,380 --> 00:27:54,821
Wait a minute.

467
00:27:54,901 --> 00:27:57,442
Hold it down.

468
00:27:57,462 --> 00:27:58,182
Harriet!

469
00:27:58,614 --> 00:27:59,715
J-P-O-J.

470
00:27:59,735 --> 00:28:00,196
What is that?

471
00:28:00,236 --> 00:28:01,157
All right.

472
00:28:01,177 --> 00:28:01,777
You know Gil.

473
00:28:01,817 --> 00:28:02,898
Yeah, how are you, Gil?

474
00:28:02,918 --> 00:28:03,299
Good to see you.

475
00:28:03,339 --> 00:28:04,000
How about a drink?

476
00:28:04,100 --> 00:28:04,380
Fine.

477
00:28:04,400 --> 00:28:05,922
In the kitchen, you'll find everything you need.

478
00:28:07,123 --> 00:28:07,563
Thanks.

479
00:28:07,924 --> 00:28:08,244
Yeah.

480
00:28:08,584 --> 00:28:11,187
So... Oh, honey, that is you.

481
00:28:11,267 --> 00:28:11,968
That is you.

482
00:28:11,988 --> 00:28:14,270
Fred, darling, I'm so glad you could come.

483
00:28:14,911 --> 00:28:15,852
I brought you a house present.

484
00:28:16,312 --> 00:28:17,173
Something to book this.

485
00:28:17,193 --> 00:28:18,234
Oh, you're sweet.

486
00:28:19,916 --> 00:28:20,156
There.

487
00:28:20,797 --> 00:28:21,818
Doesn't that look nice?

488
00:28:22,902 --> 00:28:23,783
Give me a cigarette, O.J.

489
00:28:23,843 --> 00:28:24,483
Sure, sure.

490
00:28:24,663 --> 00:28:24,883
O.J.'

491
00:28:24,863 --> 00:28:25,504
's a great agent.

492
00:28:25,524 --> 00:28:26,965
He knows a terrific lot of phone numbers.

493
00:28:27,305 --> 00:28:28,906
What's Jerry Wall's phone number, O.J.?

494
00:28:28,946 --> 00:28:29,727
Oh, come on.

495
00:28:29,747 --> 00:28:30,207
Lay off.

496
00:28:30,227 --> 00:28:32,929
Darling, I want you to call him and tell him what a genius Fred is.

497
00:28:32,949 --> 00:28:33,449
Yes, I got it.

498
00:28:34,070 --> 00:28:34,890
Stop blushing, Fred.

499
00:28:34,910 --> 00:28:35,911
You didn't say you were a genius.

500
00:28:36,011 --> 00:28:36,351
I did.

501
00:28:38,152 --> 00:28:39,353
So quit stalling, O.J.

502
00:28:39,733 --> 00:28:42,095
Just tell me what you're going to do to make Fred rich and famous.

503
00:28:42,235 --> 00:28:44,957
Now, why don't you let Fred baby me settle that matter, huh, puppy?

504
00:28:45,657 --> 00:28:47,439
Okay, but just remember, I'm the agent.

505
00:28:47,679 --> 00:28:48,860
He's already got a decorator.

506
00:28:49,080 --> 00:28:49,820
I'm the agent.

507
00:28:51,361 --> 00:28:51,902
Hold it, hold it.

508
00:28:53,978 --> 00:28:55,419
All right, boys, come on in here.

509
00:28:55,439 --> 00:28:59,401
Right in the kitchen, you'll find everything you need.

510
00:29:00,542 --> 00:29:01,663
So, listen, Fred baby.

511
00:29:01,683 --> 00:29:03,323
What do you think?

512
00:29:03,344 --> 00:29:04,084
No, it's Paul baby.

513
00:29:04,284 --> 00:29:04,624
Oh, it is?

514
00:29:04,644 --> 00:29:05,625
I thought it was Fred baby.

515
00:29:05,685 --> 00:29:05,845
No.

516
00:29:05,965 --> 00:29:06,845
Well, answer the question now.

517
00:29:06,885 --> 00:29:07,566
Is she or isn't she?

518
00:29:07,966 --> 00:29:08,887
Is she or isn't she what?

519
00:29:10,047 --> 00:29:10,427
A phony.

520
00:29:11,408 --> 00:29:11,868
I don't know.

521
00:29:12,749 --> 00:29:13,449
I don't think so.

522
00:29:13,529 --> 00:29:14,430
You don't think so, huh?

523
00:29:14,490 --> 00:29:16,031
Well, you're wrong.

524
00:29:16,611 --> 00:29:16,991
She is.

525
00:29:17,311 --> 00:29:19,072
But on the other hand, you're right.

526
00:29:19,873 --> 00:29:20,773
Because she's a real phony.

527
00:29:21,434 --> 00:29:21,854
You know why?

528
00:29:22,230 --> 00:29:24,871
because she honestly believes all this phony junk that she believes.

529
00:29:24,891 --> 00:29:25,472
I mean it.

530
00:29:25,752 --> 00:29:26,612
Now, look, I like the kid.

531
00:29:26,652 --> 00:29:28,053
I mean, I sincerely like the kid.

532
00:29:28,073 --> 00:29:28,313
I do.

533
00:29:28,673 --> 00:29:29,774
I mean, I'm sensitive, that's why.

534
00:29:29,794 --> 00:29:31,975
I mean, you gotta be sensitive to like the kid, you know what I mean?

535
00:29:32,075 --> 00:29:34,977
It's what you call a touch, a streak of the poet, you know what I mean?

536
00:29:35,697 --> 00:29:36,278
You known her long?

537
00:29:36,838 --> 00:29:37,278
Are you kidding?

538
00:29:37,298 --> 00:29:38,119
I'm the guy that discovered her.

539
00:29:38,159 --> 00:29:38,439
I'm O.J.

540
00:29:38,459 --> 00:29:38,719
Berman.

541
00:29:38,739 --> 00:29:41,440
A couple of years ago, back in the coast, she was just a kid.

542
00:29:41,620 --> 00:29:43,661
Of course, she had a lot of style, a lot of class, you know.

543
00:29:43,681 --> 00:29:44,122
A lot of what?

544
00:29:44,182 --> 00:29:44,862
Class, yeah.

545
00:29:44,882 --> 00:29:45,663
She had a lot of class.

546
00:29:46,303 --> 00:29:48,684
But, of course, when she opened her mouth, you didn't know what she was talking about.

547
00:29:48,704 --> 00:29:49,745
She was a hillbilly ranoke.

548
00:29:50,405 --> 00:29:52,006
You know how long it took me to smooth out that accent?

549
00:29:52,266 --> 00:29:53,227
No, I'll tell you how long.

550
00:29:53,947 --> 00:29:54,367
One year.

551
00:29:54,708 --> 00:29:55,248
You know how we did it?

552
00:29:56,589 --> 00:29:57,389
We gave her French lessons.

553
00:29:57,750 --> 00:30:00,572
Yeah, figured once she could imitate French, she had no trouble imitating English.

554
00:30:01,392 --> 00:30:04,234
And finally, when I thought she was be ready, I arranged for a little screen test.

555
00:30:04,454 --> 00:30:07,016
Well, the night before the screen test, well, I could have killed myself.

556
00:30:07,356 --> 00:30:08,817
The night before the screen test, the phone rings.

557
00:30:08,857 --> 00:30:09,237
I pick it up.

558
00:30:09,277 --> 00:30:10,098
I said, OJ speaking.

559
00:30:10,118 --> 00:30:10,778
She says, this is Holly.

560
00:30:10,798 --> 00:30:12,840
I said, Holly, Holly, you sound so far away, honey.

561
00:30:12,860 --> 00:30:13,320
What's with you?

562
00:30:13,340 --> 00:30:14,061
She says, I'm in New York.

563
00:30:14,081 --> 00:30:14,801
I said, what kind of New York?

564
00:30:14,821 --> 00:30:15,722
You got a screen test tomorrow.

565
00:30:15,982 --> 00:30:17,903
She says, I'm in New York because I've never been to New York before.

566
00:30:17,923 --> 00:30:19,344
I said, get yourself on the plane.

567
00:30:19,364 --> 00:30:19,865
Get back here.

568
00:30:19,885 --> 00:30:20,625
She says, I don't want it.

569
00:30:20,645 --> 00:30:21,906
I said, what do you mean you don't want it?

570
00:30:22,166 --> 00:30:22,646
What do you want?

571
00:30:22,947 --> 00:30:23,647
She says, I don't want it.

572
00:30:23,688 --> 00:30:24,648
I says, what do you want?

573
00:30:24,668 --> 00:30:26,931
She says, when I find out what I want, I'm going to let you know.

574
00:30:27,071 --> 00:30:27,331
Bang.

575
00:30:28,012 --> 00:30:30,234
So look, Fred, baby, you know, don't tell me.

576
00:30:30,254 --> 00:30:30,875
This is Paul, baby.

577
00:30:30,895 --> 00:30:31,595
Paul, baby, sure.

578
00:30:31,615 --> 00:30:33,457
I mean, don't tell me that she isn't a phony.

579
00:30:33,477 --> 00:30:34,118
You know what I mean?

580
00:30:34,518 --> 00:30:34,939
Irving.

581
00:30:35,900 --> 00:30:37,281
Honey Irving, where have you been?

582
00:30:53,538 --> 00:30:54,519
She's been running around.

583
00:31:08,034 --> 00:31:10,216
You look so wonderful.

584
00:31:10,256 --> 00:31:18,683
I know, and after all that she said about him, and he knows that I'm gonna be there, and he brings it, and she just talks like a baby.

585
00:31:18,723 --> 00:31:19,103
Molly!

586
00:31:19,163 --> 00:31:24,268
I know, especially when he does it.

587
00:31:24,368 --> 00:31:25,288
Time, darling.

588
00:31:25,389 --> 00:31:25,609
What?

589
00:31:25,689 --> 00:31:26,369
The time.

590
00:31:26,389 --> 00:31:27,630
Do you have a watch?

591
00:31:27,670 --> 00:31:28,091
No, I don't.

592
00:31:28,171 --> 00:31:29,992
Oh, let me see a minute.

593
00:31:30,072 --> 00:31:30,813
It's 6.45.

594
00:31:30,873 --> 00:31:33,435
Thank you so much, dear.

595
00:31:33,475 --> 00:31:34,216
Can I borrow it?

596
00:31:34,276 --> 00:31:36,037
I don't know how to call it.

597
00:31:39,478 --> 00:31:44,341
This is some party.

598
00:31:44,381 --> 00:31:53,688
Who are all these people, anyhow?

599
00:31:53,848 --> 00:31:54,808
Who knows?

600
00:31:54,848 --> 00:31:56,189
The word gets out.

601
00:31:56,229 --> 00:32:00,372
You don't mind, do you, darling?

602
00:32:11,175 --> 00:32:12,295
Reinforcements.

603
00:32:13,436 --> 00:32:13,776
Holly!

604
00:32:13,836 --> 00:32:15,577
Holly, my darling!

605
00:32:16,698 --> 00:32:18,139
What's that?

606
00:32:40,418 --> 00:32:41,999
Mag Wildwood.

607
00:32:43,020 --> 00:32:45,823
She's a model, believe it or not, and a thumping bore.

608
00:32:47,204 --> 00:32:49,186
But just look at the goodies she brought with her.

609
00:32:50,687 --> 00:32:57,433
She's all right, I suppose, if you like dark, handsome, rich-looking men with passionate natures and too many teeth.

610
00:32:57,813 --> 00:32:58,694
I don't mean that one.

611
00:32:58,714 --> 00:32:59,534
I mean the other one.

612
00:33:03,278 --> 00:33:04,479
The other one?

613
00:33:04,639 --> 00:33:05,900
It's Rusty Trawler.

614
00:33:06,681 --> 00:33:06,901
Hmm?

615
00:33:07,161 --> 00:33:08,322
Rusty Trawler.

616
00:33:08,914 --> 00:33:12,096
He happens to be the ninth richest man in America under 50.

617
00:33:14,038 --> 00:33:17,780
Now, that indeed is a remarkable piece of information to have at your fingertips.

618
00:33:18,160 --> 00:33:19,421
I keep track of these things.

619
00:33:21,142 --> 00:33:22,303
Excuse me, you owe me 40...

620
00:33:22,343 --> 00:33:23,544
Hold this for me, will you, darling?

621
00:33:24,324 --> 00:33:25,365
Oh, it was so romantic.

622
00:33:27,218 --> 00:33:29,079
Mag, darling, what are you doing here?

623
00:33:29,179 --> 00:33:35,262
Honey, I was upstairs working with Unioshi Easter stuff for the bazaar.

624
00:33:35,582 --> 00:33:38,563
Then these two nice boys came to pick me up.

625
00:33:38,583 --> 00:33:39,964
It was a mistaken course.

626
00:33:39,984 --> 00:33:41,845
My wires got crossed somewhere.

627
00:33:41,865 --> 00:33:44,746
They were both very sweet about it.

628
00:33:45,266 --> 00:33:48,188
May I present Jose Silva Pereira.

629
00:33:48,688 --> 00:33:50,449
He's from Brazil.

630
00:33:51,249 --> 00:33:52,510
Mr. Golatly.

631
00:33:54,966 --> 00:33:57,609
Very kind of you, Miss Godlightly, to allow me to attend your party.

632
00:33:58,090 --> 00:34:00,353
I'm so interested in North American culture.

633
00:34:01,354 --> 00:34:10,806
I've been already, of course, to the Statue of Liberty and to the Restaurant Automatique, but this is the first time I'm in a typical North American home.

634
00:34:10,946 --> 00:34:13,589
Wouldn't he just melt in your mouth?

635
00:34:14,630 --> 00:34:17,373
And this is Mr. Rusty Trawler, Mr. Colato.

636
00:34:18,113 --> 00:34:20,075
You're now vexed of me for bringing him.

637
00:34:20,215 --> 00:34:21,537
Of course not, darling.

638
00:34:21,557 --> 00:34:22,317
I'm glad.

639
00:34:22,658 --> 00:34:25,581
Now, who's going to bring me a bourbon?

640
00:34:25,801 --> 00:34:26,662
O.J.?

641
00:34:26,982 --> 00:34:28,163
Yeah.

642
00:34:28,363 --> 00:34:30,165
Would you get Miss Wildwood a drink?

643
00:34:30,185 --> 00:34:31,626
Yeah, which one's Miss Wildwood?

644
00:34:31,667 --> 00:34:31,767
Mr.

645
00:34:31,807 --> 00:34:33,248
Bourbon.

646
00:34:33,288 --> 00:34:38,733
We have been formally introduced by our mad Wildwood from Wildwood, Arkansas.

647
00:34:39,114 --> 00:34:39,374
That's...

648
00:34:39,934 --> 00:34:40,755
Hill Country.

649
00:34:40,835 --> 00:34:42,857
Now you just make yourself right at home, senor.

650
00:34:42,917 --> 00:34:44,099
Oh, do not trouble yourself.

651
00:34:44,239 --> 00:34:48,043
I'm content to stand observing the customs of your country.

652
00:34:48,343 --> 00:34:49,445
Okay, you do that.

653
00:34:49,465 --> 00:34:51,567
Now come along, Mr. Trawler.

654
00:34:51,967 --> 00:34:53,809
Let's see what we can find to amuse you with.

655
00:36:12,214 --> 00:36:16,579
and at the last minute, I had Gil call... Look, I wasn't supposed to pick you up here or any...

656
00:36:21,184 --> 00:36:21,485
Yes.

657
00:36:22,025 --> 00:36:29,734
Miss Gold, I think this time I'm warning you, I am definitely this time going to call in the police.

658
00:36:50,358 --> 00:36:51,138
Something important?

659
00:36:51,559 --> 00:36:53,259
No, just the guy upstairs.

660
00:36:53,679 --> 00:36:54,720
Complaining about the noise.

661
00:36:55,080 --> 00:36:55,580
He's angry.

662
00:36:56,961 --> 00:36:59,422
Well, he did mention something about calling the police.

663
00:36:59,582 --> 00:37:00,562
Oh, the police.

664
00:37:01,202 --> 00:37:01,683
The police.

665
00:37:02,203 --> 00:37:03,363
Oh, that I cannot have.

666
00:37:03,744 --> 00:37:05,424
I'd better look for Miss Wildwood and go.

667
00:37:09,166 --> 00:37:14,488
Do you think I'd find a bull of mine moused after a piece of cheap Hollywood crap?

668
00:37:14,768 --> 00:37:16,789
Mac, darling, you're being a bull.

669
00:37:16,829 --> 00:37:17,309
Shut up!

670
00:37:18,690 --> 00:37:19,090
You...

671
00:37:19,958 --> 00:37:21,759
You know what's gonna happen to you?

672
00:37:21,779 --> 00:37:27,323
I am gonna march you over to the zoo and feed you to the yak.

673
00:37:28,444 --> 00:37:29,905
As soon as I finish this trick.

674
00:39:16,642 --> 00:39:17,183
Good evening, Ed.

675
00:39:17,663 --> 00:39:18,424
It's Paul, baby.

676
00:39:18,444 --> 00:39:18,865
Oh, yes.

677
00:39:18,905 --> 00:39:19,866
You remember Irving, don't you?

678
00:39:19,886 --> 00:39:20,466
Yeah, hi, Irving.

679
00:39:20,526 --> 00:39:21,508
Ed, this is Jose.

680
00:39:21,608 --> 00:39:23,410
Nice to meet you, Jose.

681
00:39:23,990 --> 00:39:25,292
Wonderful seeing you.

682
00:39:25,973 --> 00:39:26,193
Yes.

683
00:39:26,213 --> 00:39:26,974
Jewel thieves.

684
00:39:55,998 --> 00:39:58,539
I have no head for figures at all.

685
00:39:59,839 --> 00:40:01,320
I'm trying desperately to save some money.

686
00:40:01,600 --> 00:40:02,460
You know, I told you.

687
00:40:03,220 --> 00:40:04,280
I just can't seem to.

688
00:40:05,401 --> 00:40:07,001
He makes me write down everything in there.

689
00:40:07,701 --> 00:40:09,382
What I get, what I spend.

690
00:40:10,682 --> 00:40:13,183
I used to have a checking account, but he made me get rid of that.

691
00:40:14,363 --> 00:40:19,445
He feels, for me anyway, that it's better to operate on a cash basis, tax-wise.

692
00:40:20,765 --> 00:40:21,545
Someday, Mr.

693
00:40:21,585 --> 00:40:24,666
Friend, you take this book, turn it into a novel,

694
00:40:25,382 --> 00:40:26,703
Everything is there.

695
00:40:26,823 --> 00:40:28,503
Just fill in a little of the details.

696
00:40:28,904 --> 00:40:30,324
Certainly would be good for some laughs.

697
00:40:31,224 --> 00:40:31,465
No.

698
00:40:32,125 --> 00:40:33,225
No, I don't think so.

699
00:40:33,826 --> 00:40:35,826
This is a book would break the heart.

700
00:40:36,907 --> 00:40:39,828
Mr. Fitzsimmons, powder room, $50.

701
00:40:40,668 --> 00:40:42,389
Less, $18.

702
00:40:42,849 --> 00:40:45,410
Repair, one black satin dress.

703
00:40:45,710 --> 00:40:47,651
Cat food, 27 cents.

704
00:40:48,251 --> 00:40:51,113
Sally, darling, please stop making me blush.

705
00:40:51,893 --> 00:40:53,393
But you're right about Jack Fitzsimmons.

706
00:40:53,413 --> 00:40:54,734
He's an absolute rat.

707
00:40:56,194 --> 00:40:58,215
I guess, of course, I don't really know anybody but rats.

708
00:40:59,596 --> 00:41:00,736
Except, of course, Fred here.

709
00:41:02,757 --> 00:41:04,418
You do think Fred is nice, don't you, Sally?

710
00:41:04,938 --> 00:41:09,280
For you, I hope he is.

711
00:41:12,482 --> 00:41:16,244
Give me a kiss goodbye, baby.

712
00:41:18,385 --> 00:41:19,425
Goodbye, Uncle Sally.

713
00:41:19,726 --> 00:41:20,346
Till next week.

714
00:41:22,550 --> 00:41:23,731
Goodbye, Uncle Sally.

715
00:41:23,751 --> 00:41:25,813
Goodbye, and don't forget to send that book, eh?

716
00:41:26,013 --> 00:41:26,313
I won't.

717
00:41:29,056 --> 00:41:30,877
Oh, what about the weather report?

718
00:41:31,998 --> 00:41:32,498
Oh, yes.

719
00:41:33,339 --> 00:41:36,562
Snow flurries expected this weekend in New Orleans.

720
00:41:41,326 --> 00:41:43,868
Snow flurries expected this weekend in New Orleans?

721
00:41:45,209 --> 00:41:46,250
Isn't that just the weirdest?

722
00:41:47,211 --> 00:41:49,913
I bet they haven't had snow in New Orleans for a million years.

723
00:41:51,230 --> 00:41:52,417
I don't know how he thinks them up.

724
00:43:43,074 --> 00:43:43,514
What are you doing?

725
00:43:44,015 --> 00:43:50,779
Writing.

726
00:43:53,660 --> 00:43:54,000
Good.

727
00:43:55,041 --> 00:43:57,522
Well, hello.

728
00:43:58,283 --> 00:43:58,763
What's wrong?

729
00:44:00,584 --> 00:44:01,065
I don't know.

730
00:44:02,105 --> 00:44:03,406
It's probably nothing.

731
00:44:03,426 --> 00:44:07,288
I want to see if he's still there.

732
00:44:08,069 --> 00:44:08,949
See if who's still there?

733
00:44:08,969 --> 00:44:10,330
What are you talking about?

734
00:44:22,904 --> 00:44:23,225
See?

735
00:44:24,365 --> 00:44:25,987
I noticed him yesterday afternoon.

736
00:44:27,067 --> 00:44:27,868
I didn't say anything.

737
00:44:27,908 --> 00:44:33,172
I didn't want to sound neurotic, but when he's there again today... Who do you think he is?

738
00:44:34,213 --> 00:44:37,515
Well, it could be anybody, of course, but what crossed my mind was...

739
00:44:39,236 --> 00:44:41,458
Suppose Bill's having us watched, huh?

740
00:44:44,922 --> 00:44:46,624
Okay, I'll take care of this.

741
00:44:47,986 --> 00:44:49,889
No, no, don't, please.

742
00:44:51,010 --> 00:44:53,814
Look, if that's what it is, you'll only make everything worse.

743
00:44:54,035 --> 00:44:54,655
I'll be careful.

744
00:44:54,976 --> 00:44:55,457
You wait here.

745
00:44:56,518 --> 00:44:57,840
Darling, please don't.

746
00:44:57,880 --> 00:44:58,941
I don't think you should.

747
00:44:58,961 --> 00:44:59,923
Now, take it easy.

748
00:44:59,943 --> 00:45:02,126
I just want to find out what this is all about.

749
00:46:49,218 --> 00:46:49,718
What do you want?

750
00:46:51,719 --> 00:46:54,101
Son, I need a friend.

751
00:47:07,008 --> 00:47:07,428
That's me.

752
00:47:08,449 --> 00:47:08,889
That's her.

753
00:47:09,849 --> 00:47:10,770
That's her brother Fred.

754
00:47:20,542 --> 00:47:21,463
You're Holly's father?

755
00:47:23,564 --> 00:47:24,464
Her name ain't Holly.

756
00:47:25,805 --> 00:47:26,886
She was Lula Mae Barnes.

757
00:47:28,026 --> 00:47:29,107
Was till she married me.

758
00:47:30,107 --> 00:47:31,448
I'm her husband, Doc O'Lightly.

759
00:47:34,810 --> 00:47:35,550
Paul Varjack.

760
00:47:37,591 --> 00:47:39,492
I'm a horse doctor, animal man.

761
00:47:40,413 --> 00:47:42,114
Do some farming, too, near Tulip, Texas.

762
00:47:44,475 --> 00:47:46,156
Her brother Fred's getting out of the army soon.

763
00:47:46,997 --> 00:47:49,498
Lula Mae belongs home with her husband, her brother, and her children.

764
00:47:50,698 --> 00:47:54,241
Them's her children?

765
00:47:55,182 --> 00:47:56,223
She's got four children.

766
00:47:57,424 --> 00:47:59,786
Now, son, I didn't claim they was her natural born children.

767
00:48:00,727 --> 00:48:07,853
Their own precious mother, precious woman, passed away July the 4th, Independence Day, 1955, the year of the drought.

768
00:48:08,874 --> 00:48:10,836
When I married Lula Mae, she was going on 14.

769
00:48:12,097 --> 00:48:17,842
Now, you might think the average person going on 14 wouldn't know the old mine, but you take Lula Mae,

770
00:48:19,402 --> 00:48:20,543
She was an exceptional person.

771
00:48:26,508 --> 00:48:29,451
I'll tell you, son, she just plumb broke our hearts when she ran off like she done.

772
00:48:30,972 --> 00:48:32,173
Just plain had no reason.

773
00:48:33,694 --> 00:48:35,195
All the housework was done by her daughters.

774
00:48:36,316 --> 00:48:38,278
Lula May could just take it easy.

775
00:48:39,179 --> 00:48:40,860
I tell you, that woman got positively fat.

776
00:48:41,541 --> 00:48:42,141
What, her brother?

777
00:48:43,222 --> 00:48:44,543
He growed up into a giant.

778
00:48:45,764 --> 00:48:47,626
Which is a sight different from the way they come to us.

779
00:48:49,150 --> 00:48:50,691
Couple of wild young'uns they was.

780
00:48:52,112 --> 00:48:54,293
I caught them outside the house stealing milk and turkey eggs.

781
00:48:55,934 --> 00:48:59,536
Zulu Mae and her brother had been living with some mean no-com people about a hundred miles east of Tulum.

782
00:49:01,017 --> 00:49:02,718
She had good cause to run off from that house.

783
00:49:03,678 --> 00:49:04,719
Never had none to leave mine.

784
00:49:08,141 --> 00:49:08,821
What about her brother?

785
00:49:08,841 --> 00:49:09,582
Didn't he leave too?

786
00:49:10,702 --> 00:49:11,082
No, sir.

787
00:49:11,583 --> 00:49:13,204
We had Fred with us till he took him in the army.

788
00:49:13,964 --> 00:49:15,325
That's what I come to talk to her about.

789
00:49:15,345 --> 00:49:16,185
I had a letter from him.

790
00:49:16,265 --> 00:49:17,486
He's getting out of the army in February.

791
00:49:18,498 --> 00:49:20,259
That's why I got on a Greyhound bus to come to get her.

792
00:49:21,140 --> 00:49:23,481
Lula Mae's place is with her husband, her children, and her brother.

793
00:49:25,022 --> 00:49:26,944
Huh?

794
00:49:28,004 --> 00:49:29,005
Surprised me, Crackerjack.

795
00:49:29,025 --> 00:49:29,405
You want it?

796
00:49:32,407 --> 00:49:32,808
Uh-uh.

797
00:49:33,108 --> 00:49:34,869
Never could understand why that woman run off.

798
00:49:37,030 --> 00:49:38,171
Don't tell me she wasn't happy.

799
00:49:39,512 --> 00:49:41,253
Talky as a jaybird, she was.

800
00:49:41,814 --> 00:49:43,275
Something smart to say on every subject.

801
00:49:44,055 --> 00:49:44,916
Better than the radio.

802
00:49:46,397 --> 00:49:48,038
And I proposed, I cried like a baby.

803
00:49:48,990 --> 00:49:50,652
She said, what do you want to cry for, Doc?

804
00:49:50,672 --> 00:49:51,633
Of course we'll be married.

805
00:49:52,153 --> 00:49:53,294
I've never been married before.

806
00:49:53,314 --> 00:49:57,959
Well, I had to laugh and hug and squeeze her.

807
00:49:58,759 --> 00:50:00,481
Never been married before.

808
00:50:04,705 --> 00:50:07,928
Listen, son, I advise you I need a friend.

809
00:50:09,269 --> 00:50:11,151
Because I don't want to surprise her or scare her or nothing.

810
00:50:12,753 --> 00:50:13,373
Be my friend.

811
00:50:13,473 --> 00:50:14,214
Let her know I'm here.

812
00:50:24,418 --> 00:50:30,605
Will you do that for me, son?

813
00:50:31,065 --> 00:50:31,886
Yeah, sure, doc.

814
00:50:31,906 --> 00:50:33,328
If that's what you want.

815
00:50:35,150 --> 00:50:35,470
Come on.

816
00:50:57,170 --> 00:50:59,112
I was supposed to be at 21 half an hour ago.

817
00:50:59,132 --> 00:51:01,213
Maybe we can have a drink or something tomorrow, huh?

818
00:51:01,974 --> 00:51:02,695
Sure, Lula Mae.

819
00:51:04,096 --> 00:51:05,116
If you're still here tomorrow.

820
00:51:10,601 --> 00:51:11,061
Oh, please.

821
00:51:11,241 --> 00:51:11,922
Where is he?

822
00:51:13,803 --> 00:51:14,303
Fred?

823
00:51:17,105 --> 00:51:17,526
Fred?

824
00:51:17,546 --> 00:51:20,908
Gosh.

825
00:51:22,109 --> 00:51:22,690
Lula Mae.

826
00:51:30,722 --> 00:51:31,823
Don't they feed you up here?

827
00:51:31,864 --> 00:51:35,849
You're so skinny.

828
00:51:38,833 --> 00:51:39,434
Hi, Doc.

829
00:51:40,155 --> 00:51:41,116
Gosh, little me.

830
00:51:42,818 --> 00:51:43,900
Kingdom come.

831
00:52:45,338 --> 00:52:45,858
What's the matter?

832
00:52:45,878 --> 00:52:46,479
Are you all right?

833
00:52:46,959 --> 00:52:47,539
I guess so.

834
00:52:48,619 --> 00:52:49,240
No, I'm not.

835
00:52:50,340 --> 00:52:51,120
Fred, will you help me?

836
00:52:51,380 --> 00:52:52,021
If I can.

837
00:52:52,121 --> 00:52:53,601
I want you to come to the bus station with us.

838
00:52:53,621 --> 00:52:54,162
Darken me.

839
00:52:54,402 --> 00:52:54,602
What?

840
00:52:54,942 --> 00:52:56,262
He still thinks I'm going back with him.

841
00:52:56,963 --> 00:52:57,543
I need support.

842
00:52:57,563 --> 00:52:58,843
I don't think I can play the scene alone.

843
00:52:59,124 --> 00:52:59,944
Holly, what can I do?

844
00:52:59,984 --> 00:53:00,664
He's your husband.

845
00:53:00,964 --> 00:53:01,825
No, he's not.

846
00:53:02,965 --> 00:53:03,445
He's not?

847
00:53:04,586 --> 00:53:06,607
It was annulled ages ago, but he just won't accept it.

848
00:53:07,287 --> 00:53:08,027
Please, Fred.

849
00:53:08,267 --> 00:53:09,588
I'll tell him you're coming to see us off.

850
00:53:10,548 --> 00:53:11,349
Don't say anything.

851
00:53:13,569 --> 00:53:14,950
Just meet us out front in about an hour.

852
00:53:15,358 --> 00:53:15,818
Please?

853
00:53:23,261 --> 00:53:24,022
You wait right here, honey.

854
00:53:24,062 --> 00:53:24,762
I'll get the bag.

855
00:53:29,564 --> 00:53:30,764
Why don't I get some magazines?

856
00:53:31,505 --> 00:53:32,205
Please, Fred.

857
00:53:32,785 --> 00:53:33,466
Don't leave me.

858
00:53:34,066 --> 00:53:34,766
Attention, please.

859
00:53:35,366 --> 00:53:36,387
Leaving from Platform 5.

860
00:53:36,427 --> 00:53:39,568
Philadelphia, Columbus, Indianapolis...

861
00:53:44,610 --> 00:53:45,631
Come on, Lula Mae, that's us.

862
00:53:46,871 --> 00:53:48,112
Doc, I'm not coming with you.

863
00:53:49,353 --> 00:53:49,573
Come on.

864
00:53:50,273 --> 00:53:52,654
Let's walk together quietly and I'll try and help you understand.

865
00:53:52,934 --> 00:53:53,995
Help me talk to him, Fred.

866
00:53:54,775 --> 00:53:55,556
That's all right, son.

867
00:53:55,636 --> 00:53:58,237
I appreciate you want to help, but it's between Lula Mae and me.

868
00:53:59,478 --> 00:53:59,938
Sure, Doc.

869
00:54:04,161 --> 00:54:05,021
I love you, Lula Mae.

870
00:54:06,582 --> 00:54:08,203
I know you do, and that's just the trouble.

871
00:54:08,803 --> 00:54:11,625
It's a mistake you always made, Doc, trying to love a wild thing.

872
00:54:12,445 --> 00:54:14,286
You were always lugging home wild things.

873
00:54:15,506 --> 00:54:20,449
Once it was a hawk with a broken wing, and another time it was a full-grown wildcat with a broken leg.

874
00:54:21,169 --> 00:54:21,709
Remember?

875
00:54:21,729 --> 00:54:25,652
Little me, there's something... You mustn't give your heart to a wild thing.

876
00:54:26,112 --> 00:54:27,773
The more you do, the stronger they get.

877
00:54:28,173 --> 00:54:31,034
Until they're strong enough to run into the woods or fly into a tree.

878
00:54:31,615 --> 00:54:33,716
And then to a higher tree, and then to the sky.

879
00:54:34,356 --> 00:54:35,517
Little me, there's something I gotta tell you.

880
00:54:35,697 --> 00:54:36,017
Excuse me?

881
00:54:37,138 --> 00:54:40,199
A couple of weeks ago, I got a letter from young Fred.

882
00:54:40,279 --> 00:54:40,660
Fred?

883
00:54:41,800 --> 00:54:42,741
He's all right, isn't he?

884
00:54:44,162 --> 00:54:44,822
Yeah, he's fine.

885
00:54:45,290 --> 00:54:47,812
I guess he's getting out of the army in February.

886
00:54:47,832 --> 00:54:48,692
That's what he wrote to tell me.

887
00:54:49,553 --> 00:54:50,173
February?

888
00:54:51,514 --> 00:54:52,715
Well, it's only four months.

889
00:54:53,135 --> 00:54:55,197
So, you see, you've got to come back with me, Lulu Mae.

890
00:54:55,217 --> 00:54:57,639
Your place is with me and your chariot and your brother.

891
00:54:57,659 --> 00:54:59,900
Doc, you've got to understand I can't come back.

892
00:55:00,200 --> 00:55:01,862
And you've got to understand what I'm trying to tell you.

893
00:55:02,742 --> 00:55:05,144
I don't want to seem like I'm pressuring you none, but now I've got to.

894
00:55:05,984 --> 00:55:11,828
If you don't come back with me, I'm going to have to write young Fred and tell him that unless he wants to look out for himself, he'd better sign up for another hitch.

895
00:55:11,928 --> 00:55:12,829
Doc, don't you do that.

896
00:55:12,849 --> 00:55:13,870
Don't you write that to him.

897
00:55:14,570 --> 00:55:16,511
I'll write to him myself and tell him I want him here with me.

898
00:55:17,232 --> 00:55:18,572
I'll take care of him, don't you worry.

899
00:55:18,793 --> 00:55:20,173
You're talking crazy, Lula Mae.

900
00:55:20,393 --> 00:55:21,814
Doc, stop calling me that.

901
00:55:21,834 --> 00:55:23,435
I'm not Lula Mae anymore.

902
00:55:30,760 --> 00:55:31,440
All right, Lula Mae.

903
00:55:33,661 --> 00:55:34,662
I guess you know what you're doing.

904
00:55:45,154 --> 00:55:46,276
Keep an eye on her, will you, son?

905
00:55:47,720 --> 00:55:49,503
At least see she eats something once in a while.

906
00:55:51,127 --> 00:55:51,488
Sure, Doc.

907
00:55:51,508 --> 00:55:51,909
So skinny.

908
00:56:16,546 --> 00:56:17,488
Please understand.

909
00:56:19,050 --> 00:56:21,594
I love you, but I'm just not Lula Mae anymore.

910
00:56:25,801 --> 00:56:26,322
I'm not.

911
00:57:04,674 --> 00:57:06,055
You know the terrible thing, Fred darling?

912
00:57:06,775 --> 00:57:08,696
I am still Lula Mae.

913
00:57:08,756 --> 00:57:12,217
14 years old, stealing turkey eggs and running through a briar patch.

914
00:57:12,237 --> 00:57:18,700
Except now I call it having the mean reds.

915
00:57:26,864 --> 00:57:28,885
Well, it's still too early to go to Tiffany's.

916
00:57:30,245 --> 00:57:31,686
I guess the next best thing is a drink.

917
00:57:33,410 --> 00:57:35,191
Yes, I very much need a drink.

918
00:57:36,332 --> 00:57:37,573
Will you buy me one, Fred, darling?

919
00:57:38,854 --> 00:57:40,215
Sure.

920
00:57:41,036 --> 00:57:42,117
Only promise me one thing.

921
00:57:42,357 --> 00:57:44,158
Don't take me home until I'm drunk.

922
00:57:47,181 --> 00:57:48,622
Until I'm very drunk indeed.

923
00:58:25,046 --> 00:58:29,330
Do you think she's talented, deeply and importantly talented?

924
00:58:29,891 --> 00:58:30,131
No.

925
00:58:31,412 --> 00:58:37,418
Amusingly and superficially talented, yes, but deeply and importantly, no.

926
00:59:03,970 --> 00:59:04,770
Hmm?

927
00:59:09,372 --> 00:59:11,012
Oh, indeed.

928
00:59:12,513 --> 00:59:14,393
Well, let me tell you something, mister.

929
00:59:15,073 --> 00:59:17,634
If I had her money, I'd be richer than she is.

930
00:59:18,174 --> 00:59:18,935
How do you figure that?

931
00:59:19,495 --> 00:59:21,315
Because I'd keep the candy store.

932
00:59:22,376 --> 00:59:25,256
Old Sally Tomato, that's my candy store.

933
00:59:25,777 --> 00:59:27,437
I'd always keep Sally.

934
00:59:27,817 --> 00:59:30,138
And that's why I'd be richer than she is.

935
00:59:33,302 --> 00:59:35,084
We'd better get a little more air.

936
00:59:35,104 --> 00:59:46,093
Tom, Dick, and Harry.

937
00:59:46,273 --> 00:59:47,595
No correction.

938
00:59:47,775 --> 00:59:49,536
Every Tom, Dick, and Sid.

939
00:59:49,656 --> 00:59:50,938
Harry was his friend.

940
00:59:52,359 --> 01:00:00,526
Anyway, every Tom, Dick, and Sid thinks that if he takes a girl to dinner, she'll just crawl up like a kitten in a little furry ball at his feet.

941
01:00:00,850 --> 01:00:01,150
Right?

942
01:00:01,951 --> 01:00:08,916
I have, by actual count, been taken to dinner by 26 different rats in the last two months.

943
01:00:08,976 --> 01:00:12,559
27 if you count Benny Shackley, who's in many ways a super rat.

944
01:00:13,120 --> 01:00:14,141
I think I forgot my key.

945
01:00:14,561 --> 01:00:15,081
Never mind.

946
01:00:15,101 --> 01:00:16,542
I just buzzed Juniyoshi.

947
01:00:16,783 --> 01:00:16,943
Oh.

948
01:00:17,924 --> 01:00:19,104
Do you want to know something funny?

949
01:00:19,305 --> 01:00:19,625
Mm-hmm?

950
01:00:20,005 --> 01:00:25,970
In spite of the fact that most of these rats fork up $50 for the powder room like little dolls...

951
01:00:26,798 --> 01:00:32,740
I find I have again, by actual count, $9 less in the old bank account than I had six months ago.

952
01:00:34,860 --> 01:00:39,942
So, my darling Fred, I have tonight made a very serious decision.

953
01:00:40,162 --> 01:00:40,742
And what is that?

954
01:00:42,922 --> 01:00:45,283
No longer will I play the field.

955
01:00:45,763 --> 01:00:46,563
Congratulations.

956
01:00:46,824 --> 01:00:50,645
The field stinks, both economically and socially.

957
01:00:51,825 --> 01:00:53,265
And I am giving it up.

958
01:00:53,505 --> 01:00:53,805
What?

959
01:00:54,706 --> 01:00:55,506
Miss Gulayli!

960
01:00:56,894 --> 01:01:03,479
and the New York State Housing Commission, and if necessary, the Board of Health.

961
01:01:03,780 --> 01:01:04,821
Quiet up there.

962
01:01:04,841 --> 01:01:06,222
You want to wake the whole house?

963
01:01:08,564 --> 01:01:26,158
As Miss Goliath was saying before she was so rudely interrupted, Miss Goliath further announces her intentions to devote her not inconsiderable talents to the immediate capture, for the purpose of matter, honey, of Mr. Rutherford.

964
01:01:28,095 --> 01:01:29,996
Rusty to his friends in his room.

965
01:01:30,016 --> 01:01:31,577
I'm sure he has many.

966
01:01:32,517 --> 01:01:33,398
Trawler.

967
01:01:34,078 --> 01:01:34,278
Who?

968
01:01:36,119 --> 01:01:36,940
Rusty Trawler.

969
01:01:37,640 --> 01:01:39,702
You met him at my party a couple of weeks ago.

970
01:01:39,962 --> 01:01:41,623
He came with Mag Wildwood.

971
01:01:42,223 --> 01:01:44,324
Not the beautiful Latin title.

972
01:01:45,005 --> 01:01:45,505
The other one.

973
01:01:45,765 --> 01:01:47,086
The one that looks like a pig.

974
01:01:49,127 --> 01:01:49,587
Remember?

975
01:01:50,188 --> 01:01:53,250
The ninth richest man in America under 50?

976
01:01:57,039 --> 01:02:01,081
Ah, do I detect a look of disapproval in your eye?

977
01:02:03,383 --> 01:02:06,485
Tough beans, buddy, because that's the way it's going to be.

978
01:02:07,005 --> 01:02:08,146
Hi, Kat.

979
01:02:08,847 --> 01:02:10,548
Holly, you're drunk.

980
01:02:11,749 --> 01:02:13,790
True.

981
01:02:14,110 --> 01:02:15,451
Absolutely true.

982
01:02:15,611 --> 01:02:16,732
Truly Buddy Rumble.

983
01:02:16,772 --> 01:02:17,693
What are you doing?

984
01:02:19,274 --> 01:02:23,957
So I think we should have a drink to the new Mrs. Rusty Trawler.

985
01:02:24,978 --> 01:02:25,158
Meet

986
01:02:25,842 --> 01:02:27,343
Hey, take it easy.

987
01:02:28,164 --> 01:02:28,784
What's the matter?

988
01:02:28,804 --> 01:02:29,865
Don't you think I can do it?

989
01:02:31,466 --> 01:02:31,907
Tell me.

990
01:02:32,507 --> 01:02:33,568
Seriously, I'm interested.

991
01:02:33,588 --> 01:02:34,529
Don't you think I can?

992
01:02:35,229 --> 01:02:36,090
You heard the doc.

993
01:02:36,671 --> 01:02:39,873
My brother gets out of the army in February and the doc won't take him back.

994
01:02:40,273 --> 01:02:41,775
So it's all up to me.

995
01:02:42,735 --> 01:02:44,417
I don't know why you don't understand.

996
01:02:44,437 --> 01:02:46,538
I need money.

997
01:02:46,558 --> 01:02:49,200
I'll do whatever I have to do to get it.

998
01:02:50,742 --> 01:02:51,062
So?

999
01:02:52,218 --> 01:02:56,181
This time next month, I'll be the new Mrs. Rusty Trawler.

1000
01:02:58,622 --> 01:03:00,283
And I think we should have a little drink to that.

1001
01:03:01,564 --> 01:03:02,565
It's all gone.

1002
01:03:03,345 --> 01:03:04,566
Isn't that too bad?

1003
01:03:07,528 --> 01:03:08,989
Got any whiskey upstairs?

1004
01:03:10,670 --> 01:03:11,410
But you've had enough.

1005
01:03:12,031 --> 01:03:14,112
Go ahead, get the whiskey.

1006
01:03:15,053 --> 01:03:16,814
I'll pay you for it.

1007
01:03:19,670 --> 01:03:20,491
Holly, please.

1008
01:03:20,511 --> 01:03:22,832
No, no, you disapprove of me.

1009
01:03:23,553 --> 01:03:26,875
And I do not accept drinks from gentlemen who disapprove of me.

1010
01:03:28,116 --> 01:03:30,818
I'll pay for my own whiskey, and don't you forget it.

1011
01:03:30,838 --> 01:03:31,579
Holly.

1012
01:03:32,380 --> 01:03:35,162
I do not accept drinks from disapproving gentlemen.

1013
01:03:36,182 --> 01:03:39,665
Especially not disapproving gentlemen who are kept by other ladies.

1014
01:03:40,866 --> 01:03:41,546
So take it.

1015
01:03:43,128 --> 01:03:46,190
You should be used to taking money from ladies by now.

1016
01:03:52,674 --> 01:03:54,355
If I were you, I'd be more careful with my money.

1017
01:03:55,896 --> 01:03:57,618
Rusty trawler is too hard a way of earning it.

1018
01:04:01,681 --> 01:04:06,304
It should take you exactly four seconds to cross from here to that door.

1019
01:04:08,005 --> 01:04:09,066
I'll give you two.

1020
01:05:27,042 --> 01:05:28,282
I came up to talk to you about it the other night.

1021
01:05:28,302 --> 01:05:34,424
Then I saw the paper and... Well, actually, I'm kind of embarrassed about it.

1022
01:05:35,004 --> 01:05:39,245
But since it concerns you, I thought I'd talk to you about it in person.

1023
01:05:40,186 --> 01:05:40,446
What?

1024
01:05:41,946 --> 01:05:42,206
What?

1025
01:05:43,507 --> 01:05:43,767
Mm-hmm.

1026
01:05:44,887 --> 01:05:46,087
Oh, the earplugs.

1027
01:05:47,748 --> 01:05:49,128
I can't go through the whole thing again.

1028
01:05:49,548 --> 01:05:51,749
Sufficient to say, I've come to make up.

1029
01:05:52,369 --> 01:05:54,810
And as an added inducement, I have all kinds of news.

1030
01:05:55,530 --> 01:05:55,970
Can I come in?

1031
01:05:58,226 --> 01:05:58,886
I guess so.

1032
01:05:59,866 --> 01:06:00,227
Just a minute.

1033
01:06:00,247 --> 01:06:01,267
Do I have a nightgown on?

1034
01:06:02,347 --> 01:06:02,827
No, I don't.

1035
01:06:03,247 --> 01:06:04,728
Would you mind turning around for a second?

1036
01:06:05,388 --> 01:06:05,948
Oh, never mind.

1037
01:06:05,968 --> 01:06:07,149
It's such a corny line anyway.

1038
01:06:07,809 --> 01:06:08,829
I'll turn it around myself.

1039
01:06:09,889 --> 01:06:10,329
Come in.

1040
01:06:12,710 --> 01:06:14,591
Have you seen the paper?

1041
01:06:15,851 --> 01:06:15,951
Mm.

1042
01:06:17,151 --> 01:06:17,892
Rusty, you mean?

1043
01:06:18,132 --> 01:06:18,372
Mm-hmm.

1044
01:06:19,072 --> 01:06:20,272
Yes, I know all about it.

1045
01:06:21,273 --> 01:06:24,013
Suddenly had him pegged wrong, didn't I?

1046
01:06:25,154 --> 01:06:26,214
I thought he was just a rat.

1047
01:06:27,238 --> 01:06:29,100
He was a super rat all along.

1048
01:06:30,201 --> 01:06:32,202
A super rat in rat clothing.

1049
01:06:33,083 --> 01:06:34,444
You don't even know the best part.

1050
01:06:35,605 --> 01:06:37,967
Not only was he a rat, but a super rat, rather.

1051
01:06:38,268 --> 01:06:39,709
He was also broke.

1052
01:06:40,770 --> 01:06:41,290
Broke?

1053
01:06:41,871 --> 01:06:43,292
I mean, but not a farthing.

1054
01:06:44,293 --> 01:06:47,896
His family has money, of course, but he personally is broke.

1055
01:06:47,936 --> 01:06:49,457
Turns out he owes $700,000.

1056
01:06:49,478 --> 01:06:50,759
Can you imagine anyone owing $700,000?

1057
01:06:55,830 --> 01:06:57,031
Forty-three dollars, yes.

1058
01:06:58,551 --> 01:07:02,113
Anyway, that's why he decided to marry the queen of the pig people.

1059
01:07:03,674 --> 01:07:05,274
I'll tell you one thing, Fred, darling.

1060
01:07:06,555 --> 01:07:08,356
I'd marry you for your money in a minute.

1061
01:07:09,176 --> 01:07:10,297
Would you marry me for my money?

1062
01:07:11,377 --> 01:07:11,797
In a minute.

1063
01:07:13,578 --> 01:07:15,399
So I guess it's pretty lucky neither of us is rich, huh?

1064
01:07:16,940 --> 01:07:17,160
Yeah.

1065
01:07:20,101 --> 01:07:22,022
Oh, Fred, darling, I'm so glad to see you.

1066
01:07:27,638 --> 01:07:28,699
What have you been doing?

1067
01:07:31,201 --> 01:07:31,941
Writing, mostly.

1068
01:07:35,004 --> 01:07:35,644
Sold a story.

1069
01:07:35,664 --> 01:07:36,905
Just got word this morning.

1070
01:07:37,806 --> 01:07:39,307
Oh, that's marvelous.

1071
01:07:39,988 --> 01:07:40,969
It really is.

1072
01:07:42,710 --> 01:07:45,773
But only how does your decorator friend feel about it?

1073
01:07:45,793 --> 01:07:48,034
I thought you were supposed to be saving yourself and all of that.

1074
01:07:49,355 --> 01:07:49,936
You know something?

1075
01:07:50,937 --> 01:07:52,678
I haven't quite got around to telling her about it yet.

1076
01:07:54,099 --> 01:07:57,462
Look, why don't we go out and have a drink or take a walk or something to celebrate?

1077
01:07:58,098 --> 01:07:58,518
All right.

1078
01:07:59,318 --> 01:08:00,819
I think there's some champagne in the icebox.

1079
01:08:01,399 --> 01:08:02,679
Why don't you open it while I get dressed?

1080
01:08:03,140 --> 01:08:03,480
Okay.

1081
01:08:05,340 --> 01:08:07,861
I don't think I've ever drunk champagne before breakfast before.

1082
01:08:09,862 --> 01:08:13,503
With breakfast on several occasions, but never before before.

1083
01:08:13,983 --> 01:08:15,743
Now, I've got a wonderful idea.

1084
01:08:16,243 --> 01:08:18,404
We could spend the whole day doing things we've never done before.

1085
01:08:18,764 --> 01:08:21,765
We'll take turns for something you've never done and me.

1086
01:08:21,825 --> 01:08:24,466
Of course, I can't really think of anything I've never done.

1087
01:08:42,786 --> 01:08:44,567
I've never been for a walk in the morning before.

1088
01:08:44,988 --> 01:08:46,329
At least not since I've been in New York.

1089
01:08:47,190 --> 01:08:50,312
I've walked up Fifth Avenue at 6, but as far as I'm concerned, that's till night.

1090
01:08:50,552 --> 01:08:51,313
Do you think it counts?

1091
01:08:51,513 --> 01:08:52,574
Sure it counts.

1092
01:08:53,115 --> 01:08:53,755
Now we're even.

1093
01:09:07,507 --> 01:09:08,628
Don't you just love it?

1094
01:09:09,408 --> 01:09:09,849
Love what?

1095
01:09:10,809 --> 01:09:11,370
Tiffany's.

1096
01:09:22,482 --> 01:09:23,402
Isn't it wonderful?

1097
01:09:24,343 --> 01:09:27,343
Do you see what I mean, how nothing bad could ever happen to you in a place like this?

1098
01:09:27,683 --> 01:09:30,424
It isn't that I give a hoot about jewelry, except diamonds, of course.

1099
01:09:32,765 --> 01:09:33,465
Like that.

1100
01:09:40,607 --> 01:09:41,287
What do you think?

1101
01:09:42,888 --> 01:09:49,150
Well... Of course, personally, I think it'd be tacky to wear diamonds before I'm 40.

1102
01:09:50,230 --> 01:09:50,770
Well, you're right.

1103
01:09:52,022 --> 01:09:53,383
But in the meantime, you should have something.

1104
01:09:53,743 --> 01:09:54,203
I'll wait.

1105
01:09:55,484 --> 01:09:56,584
No, I'm going to buy you a present.

1106
01:09:57,885 --> 01:09:59,786
You bought me one, a typewriter ribbon, and it brought me luck.

1107
01:10:00,426 --> 01:10:02,428
All right, but Tiffany's can be pretty expensive.

1108
01:10:02,608 --> 01:10:04,409
I've got my check and $10.

1109
01:10:04,569 --> 01:10:10,712
Oh, I wouldn't let you cash a check, but a present for $10 or under, that I'll accept.

1110
01:10:11,812 --> 01:10:13,894
Of course, I don't exactly know what we're going to find at Tiffany's for $10.

1111
01:10:25,462 --> 01:10:26,102
May I help you?

1112
01:10:27,202 --> 01:10:27,643
Perhaps.

1113
01:10:28,143 --> 01:10:29,903
Actually, we were looking for a present for the lady.

1114
01:10:30,203 --> 01:10:30,804
Certainly, sir.

1115
01:10:31,404 --> 01:10:33,144
Is there something special you had in mind?

1116
01:10:34,865 --> 01:10:36,625
Well, we had considered diamonds.

1117
01:10:37,525 --> 01:10:41,567
Now, I don't want to offend you, but the lady feels that diamonds are tacky for her.

1118
01:10:43,387 --> 01:10:47,008
Oh, I think they're divine on older women, but I don't think they'd be right for me.

1119
01:10:47,028 --> 01:10:47,808
You do understand.

1120
01:10:48,388 --> 01:10:48,809
Certainly.

1121
01:10:49,409 --> 01:10:53,750
In all fairness, I think I ought to explain there is also a secondary problem.

1122
01:10:54,226 --> 01:10:55,007
one of finance.

1123
01:10:55,047 --> 01:10:58,329
We can only afford to spend a limited amount.

1124
01:10:58,970 --> 01:11:00,591
May I ask how limited?

1125
01:11:01,151 --> 01:11:01,752
Ten dollars.

1126
01:11:03,433 --> 01:11:04,033
Ten dollars.

1127
01:11:05,374 --> 01:11:06,675
That was the outside figure, yes.

1128
01:11:07,756 --> 01:11:08,136
I see.

1129
01:11:09,337 --> 01:11:10,558
Do you have anything for ten dollars?

1130
01:11:11,219 --> 01:11:15,522
Well, frankly, madam, within that price range, the variety of merchandise is rather limited.

1131
01:11:16,302 --> 01:11:21,226
However, I do think we might have, let me see, strictly as a novelty, you understand?

1132
01:11:22,038 --> 01:11:30,583
for the lady and gentleman who has everything, a sterling silver telephone dialer at 675, including tax.

1133
01:11:31,444 --> 01:11:33,325
Sterling silver telephone dialer?

1134
01:11:33,825 --> 01:11:37,147
Yes, at 675, including federal tax.

1135
01:11:37,788 --> 01:11:46,133
Well, the price is right, but I must say I'd rather hope for something slightly more, how shall I say it, romantic in feeling.

1136
01:11:47,114 --> 01:11:47,614
What do you think?

1137
01:11:48,350 --> 01:11:53,654
As sterling silver telephone dialers go, I certainly think it's handsome, but you do understand.

1138
01:11:55,075 --> 01:11:59,118
Well, we tried, but I guess we could have something engraved, couldn't we?

1139
01:12:00,098 --> 01:12:01,219
Yes, I suppose so.

1140
01:12:02,000 --> 01:12:02,680
Yes, indeed.

1141
01:12:03,581 --> 01:12:10,726
The only problem is you would more or less have to buy something first, if only in order to have some object upon which to place the engraving.

1142
01:12:11,686 --> 01:12:12,567
You see the difficulty?

1143
01:12:13,868 --> 01:12:16,710
Well, we could have this engraved, couldn't we?

1144
01:12:17,630 --> 01:12:18,731
I think would be very smart.

1145
01:12:20,973 --> 01:12:23,655
This, I take it, was not purchased at Tiffany's?

1146
01:12:24,376 --> 01:12:24,616
No.

1147
01:12:25,436 --> 01:12:33,223
Actually, it was purchased concurrent with, uh... well, actually came inside of... well, a box of Cracker Jack.

1148
01:12:34,204 --> 01:12:34,564
I see.

1149
01:12:36,886 --> 01:12:39,788
Do they still really have prizes in Cracker Jack boxes?

1150
01:12:40,629 --> 01:12:40,969
Oh, yes.

1151
01:12:41,650 --> 01:12:42,491
That's nice to know.

1152
01:12:44,552 --> 01:12:46,494
It gives one a feeling of solidarity.

1153
01:12:47,602 --> 01:12:50,164
almost of continuity with the past that sort of thing.

1154
01:12:50,744 --> 01:12:52,846
do you think Tiffany's would really engrave it for us?

1155
01:12:53,406 --> 01:12:55,768
I mean you don't think they would feel it was beneath them or anything like that?

1156
01:12:56,428 --> 01:12:57,929
well it is rather unusual madam.

1157
01:12:59,270 --> 01:13:01,792
but I think you'll find that Tiffany's is very understanding.

1158
01:13:02,873 --> 01:13:07,116
if you will tell me what initials you would like I think we could have something ready for you in the morning.

1159
01:13:09,897 --> 01:13:12,659
didn't I tell you this was a lovely place?

1160
01:13:34,850 --> 01:13:35,950
What is this place, anyway?

1161
01:13:36,390 --> 01:13:37,551
You said you wanted to sit down.

1162
01:13:37,591 --> 01:13:38,711
It's the public library.

1163
01:13:39,471 --> 01:13:40,132
You never been here?

1164
01:13:40,552 --> 01:13:40,872
No.

1165
01:13:41,392 --> 01:13:42,272
That makes two for me.

1166
01:13:43,352 --> 01:13:44,213
I don't see any books.

1167
01:13:44,653 --> 01:13:45,313
They're in there.

1168
01:13:56,576 --> 01:13:59,517
Each one of these little drawers is stuffed with little cards.

1169
01:13:59,957 --> 01:14:02,598
And each little card is a book or an author.

1170
01:14:05,802 --> 01:14:08,524
D-A-R-J-A-K. Really?

1171
01:14:11,665 --> 01:14:12,046
Look.

1172
01:14:12,966 --> 01:14:14,107
Isn't it marvelous?

1173
01:14:14,747 --> 01:14:17,128
There you are, right in the public library.

1174
01:14:17,909 --> 01:14:20,130
Bar Jack Paul, nine lives.

1175
01:14:20,150 --> 01:14:22,031
I mean, a lot of numbers.

1176
01:14:22,812 --> 01:14:24,333
Do you think they really have the book itself?

1177
01:14:24,853 --> 01:14:25,393
Live?

1178
01:14:25,413 --> 01:14:26,754
Do you follow me?

1179
01:14:37,986 --> 01:14:38,266
57.

1180
01:14:38,566 --> 01:14:40,828
That's us.

1181
01:14:40,848 --> 01:14:41,548
57, please.

1182
01:14:41,768 --> 01:14:43,329
Nine Lives by Varjak Paul.

1183
01:14:44,610 --> 01:14:45,250
Did you ever read it?

1184
01:14:45,270 --> 01:14:46,471
It's absolutely marvelous.

1185
01:14:46,511 --> 01:14:47,431
No, I'm afraid I haven't.

1186
01:14:47,552 --> 01:14:48,272
Well, you should.

1187
01:14:49,072 --> 01:14:49,613
He wrote it.

1188
01:14:50,273 --> 01:14:51,814
He's Varjak Paul in person.

1189
01:14:53,075 --> 01:14:53,935
She doesn't believe me.

1190
01:14:55,096 --> 01:14:58,118
Show her your driver's license or Dinah's Club card or something.

1191
01:14:59,198 --> 01:15:00,439
Honest, he really is the author.

1192
01:15:00,739 --> 01:15:02,020
Cross my heart and kiss my elbow.

1193
01:15:02,300 --> 01:15:04,281
Would you kindly lower your voice, miss?

1194
01:15:04,521 --> 01:15:05,722
Why don't you autograph to them?

1195
01:15:06,398 --> 01:15:07,219
You think it'd be nice?

1196
01:15:07,459 --> 01:15:08,480
Sort of make it more personal?

1197
01:15:08,841 --> 01:15:09,641
Really, miss.

1198
01:15:10,582 --> 01:15:11,123
Go ahead.

1199
01:15:11,403 --> 01:15:12,204
Don't be so stuck up.

1200
01:15:12,384 --> 01:15:13,565
Autograph it to them.

1201
01:15:13,586 --> 01:15:13,826
All right.

1202
01:15:13,846 --> 01:15:14,887
What shall I say?

1203
01:15:16,108 --> 01:15:17,450
Something sentimental, I think.

1204
01:15:18,411 --> 01:15:19,552
What are you doing?

1205
01:15:19,772 --> 01:15:20,272
Stop that!

1206
01:15:20,332 --> 01:15:20,413
Shh!

1207
01:15:20,693 --> 01:15:22,455
You're defacing public property.

1208
01:15:22,815 --> 01:15:24,297
Well, all right, if that's the way you feel.

1209
01:15:24,977 --> 01:15:26,319
Come on, Fred Dung, let's get out of here.

1210
01:15:27,160 --> 01:15:29,001
I don't think this place is half as nice as Tiffany's.

1211
01:15:44,258 --> 01:15:46,340
Hey, did you ever steal anything from a 5 and 10?

1212
01:15:46,741 --> 01:15:47,621
When you were a kid, I mean.

1213
01:15:47,641 --> 01:15:48,522
No.

1214
01:15:49,063 --> 01:15:50,424
I'm the sensitive, bookish type.

1215
01:15:51,005 --> 01:15:51,325
Did you?

1216
01:15:52,066 --> 01:15:52,526
I used to.

1217
01:15:53,027 --> 01:15:55,349
I still do every now and then, sort of to keep my hand in.

1218
01:15:56,250 --> 01:15:57,191
Come on, don't be chicken.

1219
01:15:57,912 --> 01:15:59,413
Anyway, you've never done it, and it's your turn.

1220
01:20:26,802 --> 01:20:27,425
the house at home?

1221
01:20:29,976 --> 01:20:30,237
Ollie?

1222
01:21:04,830 --> 01:21:05,711
You know that, don't you?

1223
01:21:05,731 --> 01:21:06,851
But I love you anyway.

1224
01:21:07,512 --> 01:21:07,932
Tooie.

1225
01:21:08,832 --> 01:21:09,072
Yeah?

1226
01:21:10,473 --> 01:21:12,374
I've got to talk to you.

1227
01:21:14,154 --> 01:21:14,595
All right.

1228
01:21:14,635 --> 01:21:20,877
You want a drink?

1229
01:21:22,298 --> 01:21:22,498
Uh-uh.

1230
01:21:27,860 --> 01:21:31,862
This is going to be a serious discussion, and suddenly I'm terribly afraid it is.

1231
01:21:33,034 --> 01:21:35,035
You're going to have to take off that ridiculous mask.

1232
01:21:36,136 --> 01:21:37,617
Or else I'm going to have to wear one, too.

1233
01:21:39,138 --> 01:21:41,100
Tuli, look, please.

1234
01:21:47,044 --> 01:21:47,625
What's the matter?

1235
01:21:47,645 --> 01:21:49,466
Girl trouble?

1236
01:21:49,486 --> 01:21:52,208
Is that it, darling?

1237
01:21:55,630 --> 01:21:56,531
Oh, I see.

1238
01:21:59,333 --> 01:22:00,514
Well, that's not so serious.

1239
01:22:02,198 --> 01:22:03,599
As a matter of fact, I've been expecting it.

1240
01:22:03,619 --> 01:22:06,382
I can't say I like it, but I've been expecting it.

1241
01:22:08,924 --> 01:22:09,484
Who is she?

1242
01:22:10,105 --> 01:22:11,146
Hasn't got anything to do with her.

1243
01:22:11,166 --> 01:22:15,549
This is between you and me.

1244
01:22:16,530 --> 01:22:18,011
Oh, then it is serious.

1245
01:22:20,113 --> 01:22:20,534
Well, now.

1246
01:22:21,214 --> 01:22:22,655
Louie, you're a very stylish girl.

1247
01:22:24,897 --> 01:22:26,118
Can't we end this stylishly?

1248
01:22:28,600 --> 01:22:28,941
End it?

1249
01:22:30,162 --> 01:22:30,402
Yes.

1250
01:22:37,557 --> 01:22:41,040
I do believe love has found Andy Hardy.

1251
01:22:42,541 --> 01:22:43,261
Let's see.

1252
01:22:44,062 --> 01:22:44,702
A waitress?

1253
01:22:45,983 --> 01:22:46,844
A sales girl?

1254
01:22:47,564 --> 01:22:47,664
No.

1255
01:22:48,325 --> 01:22:50,306
She'd have to be someone rich, wouldn't she, Paul?

1256
01:22:52,307 --> 01:22:53,368
Someone who could help you.

1257
01:22:54,649 --> 01:23:01,754
Curiously enough, she's a girl who can't help anyone, not even herself.

1258
01:23:03,458 --> 01:23:04,999
The thing is, I can help her.

1259
01:23:05,019 --> 01:23:07,460
And it's a nice feeling for a change.

1260
01:23:10,761 --> 01:23:11,241
All right.

1261
01:23:13,322 --> 01:23:14,282
I understand.

1262
01:23:17,964 --> 01:23:19,104
I'll tell you what, Paul.

1263
01:23:20,105 --> 01:23:22,446
I am a very stylish girl.

1264
01:23:22,626 --> 01:23:23,326
What are you doing?

1265
01:23:24,067 --> 01:23:25,027
Writing a check.

1266
01:23:28,308 --> 01:23:29,289
Don't look so bewildered.

1267
01:23:30,049 --> 01:23:32,250
Surely you've noticed me writing checks before.

1268
01:23:33,058 --> 01:23:35,820
Pay to the order of poor Varjack $1,000.

1269
01:23:35,900 --> 01:23:39,122
Take her away somewhere for a week.

1270
01:23:41,564 --> 01:23:43,285
You're entitled to a vacation with pay.

1271
01:23:44,226 --> 01:23:46,167
Simply a matter of fair labor practice, darling.

1272
01:23:46,187 --> 01:23:50,750
Of course, if you were really smart, you'd get some of the other boys together and organize a union.

1273
01:23:51,491 --> 01:23:53,532
That way you'd get all the fringe benefits.

1274
01:23:54,273 --> 01:24:01,458
Hospitalization, a pension plan, and unemployment insurance when you're... How shall I put it?

1275
01:24:07,940 --> 01:24:09,300
Thanks for making it easier for me.

1276
01:24:11,360 --> 01:24:12,941
Don't be ridiculous, darling.

1277
01:24:13,981 --> 01:24:16,762
Take the check and call your girl.

1278
01:24:17,422 --> 01:24:17,922
No, thanks.

1279
01:24:19,523 --> 01:24:20,483
I got a check of my own.

1280
01:24:23,724 --> 01:24:28,125
When you get yourself a new rider to help, try and find one my size.

1281
01:24:29,385 --> 01:24:31,286
That way, you won't have to even shorten the sleeves.

1282
01:24:35,714 --> 01:24:35,925
Thank you.

1283
01:25:40,738 --> 01:25:42,986
Looks like a girl I know named Holly.

1284
01:25:43,307 --> 01:25:43,910
Really?

1285
01:25:46,640 --> 01:25:47,061
I'm sorry.

1286
01:26:32,392 --> 01:26:32,872
I'm busy.

1287
01:26:36,215 --> 01:26:36,795
What are you doing?

1288
01:26:37,656 --> 01:26:37,996
Reading.

1289
01:26:39,597 --> 01:26:41,778
South America, land of wealth and promise.

1290
01:26:43,519 --> 01:26:44,380
It's very interesting.

1291
01:26:47,082 --> 01:26:47,822
Let's get out of here.

1292
01:26:50,624 --> 01:26:51,505
I said, let's get out of here.

1293
01:26:51,525 --> 01:26:52,325
I want to talk to you.

1294
01:26:53,286 --> 01:26:53,606
Shh.

1295
01:26:56,548 --> 01:26:57,609
What's the matter with you anyway?

1296
01:26:58,630 --> 01:26:59,130
What's happened?

1297
01:27:01,250 --> 01:27:03,091
Fred, would you please just leave me alone?

1298
01:27:04,412 --> 01:27:07,054
Holly, I love you.

1299
01:27:12,979 --> 01:27:15,401
Where are you going?

1300
01:27:16,161 --> 01:27:16,882
To the ladies' room.

1301
01:27:20,404 --> 01:27:21,545
What's the matter with you anyway?

1302
01:27:21,805 --> 01:27:22,326
Let me go.

1303
01:27:22,446 --> 01:27:22,686
No.

1304
01:27:22,966 --> 01:27:23,847
Fred, please let me go.

1305
01:27:24,267 --> 01:27:25,248
Let's get something straight.

1306
01:27:25,788 --> 01:27:27,910
I am not now, nor have I ever been Fred.

1307
01:27:28,394 --> 01:27:30,355
Nor am I Benny Shacklett, whoever he may be.

1308
01:27:30,415 --> 01:27:31,276
My name is Paul.

1309
01:27:31,576 --> 01:27:32,877
Paul Varjack, and I love you.

1310
01:27:32,897 --> 01:27:34,038
Let me go.

1311
01:27:34,278 --> 01:27:35,199
Not till we get this settled.

1312
01:27:35,219 --> 01:27:36,880
Now, what's all this jazz about South America?

1313
01:27:38,941 --> 01:27:42,063
I thought if I'm going to marry a South American, I'd better find out something about the country.

1314
01:27:43,304 --> 01:27:43,724
Marry?

1315
01:27:43,824 --> 01:27:44,925
What South American?

1316
01:27:45,105 --> 01:27:45,626
Jose.

1317
01:27:46,406 --> 01:27:47,387
Who the hell's Jose?

1318
01:27:47,627 --> 01:27:48,828
Jose de Silva Pereira.

1319
01:27:48,848 --> 01:27:50,629
Who the hell's Jose de Silva Pereira?

1320
01:27:50,649 --> 01:27:51,429
Darling, you met him.

1321
01:27:51,469 --> 01:27:52,090
I know you did.

1322
01:27:52,170 --> 01:27:53,331
Mag Wildwood's friend.

1323
01:27:53,771 --> 01:27:55,932
The tall, good-looking one who came to the party with Rusty.

1324
01:27:56,813 --> 01:27:58,154
Well, my dear, you won't believe this.

1325
01:27:58,562 --> 01:28:03,726
But it turns out not only is he handsome and wildly rich, he's absolutely cuckoo for me.

1326
01:28:04,806 --> 01:28:05,447
You're crazy.

1327
01:28:05,467 --> 01:28:07,108
What, do you think you own me?

1328
01:28:07,268 --> 01:28:08,329
That's exactly what I think.

1329
01:28:08,749 --> 01:28:09,189
I know, I know.

1330
01:28:09,209 --> 01:28:10,510
That's what everybody always thinks.

1331
01:28:10,530 --> 01:28:11,951
But everybody happens to be wrong.

1332
01:28:11,971 --> 01:28:13,232
Well, I am not everybody.

1333
01:28:16,354 --> 01:28:16,854
Or am I?

1334
01:28:19,656 --> 01:28:20,737
Is that what you really think?

1335
01:28:20,757 --> 01:28:25,380
That I'm no different from all your other rats and super rats?

1336
01:28:27,342 --> 01:28:27,802
Wait a minute.

1337
01:28:29,602 --> 01:28:36,888
If that's it, if that's what you really think, there's something I want to give you.

1338
01:28:41,691 --> 01:28:42,272
What's that?

1339
01:28:43,933 --> 01:28:44,954
$50 for the powder room.

1340
01:29:19,718 --> 01:29:21,639
in such a mess I couldn't bear to face it alone.

1341
01:29:21,659 --> 01:29:22,579
No, you have a message.

1342
01:29:23,679 --> 01:29:23,899
No.

1343
01:29:28,560 --> 01:29:28,921
Olly.

1344
01:29:47,006 --> 01:29:48,206
Good evening, Mr. Yunioshi.

1345
01:29:54,207 --> 01:29:54,648
Good evening.

1346
01:29:57,211 --> 01:30:10,185
Please, you must leave.

1347
01:31:24,802 --> 01:31:25,282
What did you do to her?

1348
01:31:25,802 --> 01:31:26,303
Nothing.

1349
01:31:27,063 --> 01:31:28,403
There was a telegram and then this.

1350
01:31:29,184 --> 01:31:31,224
Crushing everything, conducting like a crazy.

1351
01:31:31,244 --> 01:31:32,005
It's appalling.

1352
01:31:32,505 --> 01:31:33,785
I can't have a public scandal.

1353
01:31:33,846 --> 01:31:34,706
It's too delicate.

1354
01:31:35,646 --> 01:31:37,287
I name my position, my family.

1355
01:31:38,187 --> 01:31:39,628
Will there be the police again, you think?

1356
01:31:39,948 --> 01:31:40,548
I don't see why.

1357
01:31:40,568 --> 01:31:42,689
There's no law against busting up your own apartment.

1358
01:31:43,409 --> 01:31:44,270
Where is the telegram?

1359
01:31:44,810 --> 01:31:45,290
There it is.

1360
01:31:53,570 --> 01:31:55,210
Received notice young Fred killed.

1361
01:31:59,292 --> 01:32:01,332
In a jeep accident, Fort Riley, Kansas.

1362
01:32:03,413 --> 01:32:06,574
Your husband and children joined in the sorrow of our mutual loss.

1363
01:32:08,935 --> 01:32:10,535
Letter following, Love Doc.

1364
01:32:13,216 --> 01:32:13,896
Brother Fred.

1365
01:32:14,917 --> 01:32:15,057
Oh.

1366
01:32:17,357 --> 01:32:18,978
His brother, was she very close to him?

1367
01:32:19,458 --> 01:32:19,718
Yeah.

1368
01:32:24,386 --> 01:32:25,166
What can one do?

1369
01:32:26,547 --> 01:32:27,187
Try to help her.

1370
01:32:28,408 --> 01:32:28,828
I tried.

1371
01:32:28,888 --> 01:32:29,748
It didn't do much good.

1372
01:32:32,489 --> 01:32:34,210
You got a ranch or something down in Brazil, don't you?

1373
01:32:34,890 --> 01:32:35,171
Yes.

1374
01:32:35,191 --> 01:32:37,612
That's good.

1375
01:32:37,652 --> 01:32:38,292
She'll like that.

1376
01:32:42,074 --> 01:32:42,954
Well, you better get in there.

1377
01:33:36,630 --> 01:33:40,373
The three children are still children.

1378
01:33:41,013 --> 01:33:44,515
Mrs. Magalhães is Mrs. Magalhães' wife.

1379
01:33:44,535 --> 01:33:48,718
Mr. Magalhães is Mrs. Magalhães' husband.

1380
01:34:07,166 --> 01:34:07,666
Hi.

1381
01:34:07,786 --> 01:34:08,487
Hello.

1382
01:34:08,607 --> 01:34:13,469
I got your wire.

1383
01:34:13,889 --> 01:34:17,330
How'd you know where to reach me?

1384
01:34:17,730 --> 01:34:18,631
Oh, I tried everything.

1385
01:34:19,091 --> 01:34:21,632
Called people, asked around, and suddenly thought of looking in the phone book.

1386
01:34:21,752 --> 01:34:23,033
Anyway, I'm glad you could come.

1387
01:34:26,854 --> 01:34:27,394
You look fine.

1388
01:34:27,674 --> 01:34:28,175
You think so?

1389
01:34:28,655 --> 01:34:30,816
Oh, I'm fat as a pig, and I haven't had my hair done in months.

1390
01:34:30,956 --> 01:34:32,616
But I'm happy, really happy.

1391
01:34:33,057 --> 01:34:33,797
Probably shows.

1392
01:34:34,457 --> 01:34:36,198
You look très distingué yourself.

1393
01:34:36,766 --> 01:34:37,266
I've got a job.

1394
01:34:37,566 --> 01:34:38,326
I've been writing a little.

1395
01:34:38,526 --> 01:34:38,787
I know.

1396
01:34:38,827 --> 01:34:40,007
I've read three of your stories.

1397
01:34:40,347 --> 01:34:46,349
Two in the New Yorker and one in that funny little magazine.

1398
01:34:46,449 --> 01:34:47,169
Won't you sit down?

1399
01:34:47,189 --> 01:34:48,569
Thank you.

1400
01:34:49,890 --> 01:34:50,770
I've taken up knitting.

1401
01:34:51,250 --> 01:34:51,750
So I see.

1402
01:34:51,770 --> 01:34:57,652
Probably look, uh, very nice once it's finished.

1403
01:34:57,672 --> 01:34:58,912
Actually, I'm a little nervous about it.

1404
01:34:59,272 --> 01:35:01,633
Jose brought up the blueprints for a new ranch house he's building.

1405
01:35:02,153 --> 01:35:05,714
I have this strange feeling that maybe the blueprints and my knitting instructions got switched.

1406
01:35:06,070 --> 01:35:08,272
I mean, it isn't impossible that I'm knitting a ranch house.

1407
01:35:09,392 --> 01:35:13,515
Really, darling, I can't tell you how divinely happy I am.

1408
01:35:13,635 --> 01:35:14,556
What is that, anyhow?

1409
01:35:14,576 --> 01:35:14,896
Portuguese.

1410
01:35:14,936 --> 01:35:16,117
A very complicated language.

1411
01:35:16,157 --> 01:35:17,058
4,000 irregular verbs.

1412
01:35:17,078 --> 01:35:18,699
I think you're liking...

1413
01:35:37,080 --> 01:35:37,760
Very impressive.

1414
01:35:37,780 --> 01:35:38,281
What's it mean?

1415
01:35:38,861 --> 01:35:40,402
I believe you are in league with the butcher.

1416
01:35:45,446 --> 01:35:46,287
Holly, what's this about?

1417
01:35:48,849 --> 01:35:49,710
Why did you want to see me?

1418
01:35:49,730 --> 01:35:54,954
Jose's in Washington for the night, so I thought if I asked you over, you might come.

1419
01:35:56,055 --> 01:35:58,777
Well, I've said goodbye to everyone else I care about.

1420
01:35:59,338 --> 01:36:00,078
You're going somewhere?

1421
01:36:01,019 --> 01:36:02,020
I'm going to Rio tomorrow.

1422
01:36:02,300 --> 01:36:04,502
I've got the plane ticket, and I've even said goodbye to old Sally.

1423
01:36:05,142 --> 01:36:06,222
Jose's flying down with you.

1424
01:36:06,482 --> 01:36:08,103
Well, we're going on separate planes, of course.

1425
01:36:08,463 --> 01:36:10,563
He doesn't think it would look right for us to be traveling together.

1426
01:36:11,084 --> 01:36:14,004
His family's very important down there, so he has to worry about things like that.

1427
01:36:14,965 --> 01:36:17,205
Anyway, I thought I'd show off and cook dinner for us.

1428
01:36:17,505 --> 01:36:18,466
It'll be fun eating in.

1429
01:36:19,546 --> 01:36:22,307
Did I tell you how divinely and utterly happy I am?

1430
01:36:22,667 --> 01:36:22,907
Yes.

1431
01:36:24,407 --> 01:36:25,407
You are getting married, then?

1432
01:36:26,888 --> 01:36:29,409
Well, he hasn't really asked me, not in so many words.

1433
01:36:30,189 --> 01:36:30,749
Four, you mean?

1434
01:36:31,469 --> 01:36:31,669
Huh?

1435
01:36:33,330 --> 01:36:34,670
Well, that's how many words it takes.

1436
01:36:35,762 --> 01:36:37,544
Will you marry me?

1437
01:36:38,885 --> 01:36:39,966
Oh, we'll get married, all right.

1438
01:36:40,086 --> 01:36:40,727
I know we will.

1439
01:36:41,107 --> 01:36:43,129
And in church and with his family there and everything.

1440
01:36:43,169 --> 01:36:45,571
And that's why he's waiting till we get to Rio.

1441
01:36:46,192 --> 01:36:46,652
Probably.

1442
01:36:50,376 --> 01:36:51,877
Do you think it's trying to tell us something?

1443
01:36:53,599 --> 01:36:56,521
I hope you like chicken and saffron rice served with chocolate sauce.

1444
01:36:56,982 --> 01:36:58,583
It's an East Indian classic, my dear.

1445
01:36:58,863 --> 01:37:00,725
Three months ago, I couldn't scramble eggs.

1446
01:37:07,534 --> 01:37:10,777
Oh, golly, darling, I did so want to impress you.

1447
01:37:10,797 --> 01:37:14,519
Look, I'm not much for chicken with sauce anyway.

1448
01:37:14,539 --> 01:37:16,121
Why don't we go out somewhere?

1449
01:37:16,141 --> 01:37:17,622
Let me buy you a farewell dinner.

1450
01:37:19,003 --> 01:37:19,903
Oh, that would be fun.

1451
01:37:20,444 --> 01:37:22,245
As long as it's someplace I can go like this.

1452
01:37:22,265 --> 01:37:34,254
Years from now, years and years, I'll be back.

1453
01:37:35,538 --> 01:37:37,279
Me and my nine Brazilian brats.

1454
01:37:39,280 --> 01:37:44,123
They'll be dark like Jose, of course, but they'll have bright, green, beautiful eyes.

1455
01:37:46,965 --> 01:37:50,406
I'll bring them back, all right, because they must see this.

1456
01:37:50,426 --> 01:37:53,048
Oh, I love New York.

1457
01:37:53,768 --> 01:37:54,489
Then why are you leaving?

1458
01:37:55,349 --> 01:37:56,430
What's in it for you anyway?

1459
01:37:59,031 --> 01:38:00,912
Look, I know what you're thinking, and I don't blame you.

1460
01:38:01,613 --> 01:38:03,694
I've always thrown out such a jazzy line.

1461
01:38:04,866 --> 01:38:08,687
But really, except for darken yourself, Jose's my first non-rat romance.

1462
01:38:10,207 --> 01:38:12,348
Not that he's my idea of the absolute finito.

1463
01:38:13,428 --> 01:38:15,789
He's too prim and cautious to be my absolute ideal.

1464
01:38:17,930 --> 01:38:20,510
Now, if I could choose from anybody alive, I wouldn't pick Jose.

1465
01:38:21,190 --> 01:38:22,971
Nero, maybe, or Albert Schweitzer.

1466
01:38:24,431 --> 01:38:25,372
Or Lennon Bernstein.

1467
01:38:28,072 --> 01:38:29,533
But I am mad about Jose.

1468
01:38:29,953 --> 01:38:32,013
I honestly think I'd give up smoking if he asked me.

1469
01:38:35,798 --> 01:38:36,841
Come on, darling, let's eat.

1470
01:38:37,483 --> 01:38:38,064
It's getting late.

1471
01:38:40,651 --> 01:38:42,918
I'm leaving tomorrow and I haven't even begun to pack.

1472
01:39:04,514 --> 01:39:07,076
The one who's here to think I'm the kind of girl who loses her pee.

1473
01:39:07,817 --> 01:39:09,358
So I have 26 of them made.

1474
01:39:10,539 --> 01:39:11,740
No, wait, I've got a better idea.

1475
01:39:12,380 --> 01:39:13,742
Kind of a farewell gesture.

1476
01:39:16,284 --> 01:39:17,465
Somebody must have tripped the lock.

1477
01:39:17,965 --> 01:39:19,726
Ah, crafty devil, that Yuniyoshi.

1478
01:39:20,447 --> 01:39:21,568
Wake up, wake up!

1479
01:39:21,588 --> 01:39:22,849
The British are coming!

1480
01:39:22,989 --> 01:39:24,370
Or, in this case, the Brazilians.

1481
01:39:24,530 --> 01:39:25,071
Exactly.

1482
01:39:25,271 --> 01:39:25,591
Exactly.

1483
01:39:25,811 --> 01:39:28,614
Oh, I forgot to clean up all that rice.

1484
01:39:29,775 --> 01:39:31,556
Hey, you know... Hey!

1485
01:39:33,738 --> 01:39:33,838
Ah!

1486
01:39:34,102 --> 01:39:35,283
There she are, Lieutenant!

1487
01:39:35,523 --> 01:39:36,703
The Walter woman!

1488
01:39:37,044 --> 01:39:37,424
There!

1489
01:39:39,105 --> 01:39:39,685
Kronberger.

1490
01:39:39,925 --> 01:39:40,846
Narcotic squad.

1491
01:39:41,166 --> 01:39:42,086
What do you mean?

1492
01:39:42,207 --> 01:39:43,467
What's going on?

1493
01:39:43,507 --> 01:39:44,888
Sorry, tomato!

1494
01:39:44,908 --> 01:39:48,230
They smoke more than tomatoes!

1495
01:39:48,270 --> 01:39:50,551
Look around for narcotic!

1496
01:39:50,571 --> 01:39:52,232
They've got brandy narcotic in that!

1497
01:39:54,613 --> 01:39:55,534
Come on!

1498
01:40:11,400 --> 01:40:12,200
What's your name?

1499
01:40:12,320 --> 01:40:13,020
Varjak.

1500
01:40:13,481 --> 01:40:14,701
Hold it down over there!

1501
01:40:14,861 --> 01:40:16,121
Paul Varjak.

1502
01:40:16,241 --> 01:40:18,202
V-A-R-J-A-K. Hey!

1503
01:40:18,302 --> 01:40:19,602
Now get lost, will ya?

1504
01:40:19,662 --> 01:40:21,163
Get out!

1505
01:40:21,543 --> 01:40:22,163
I'm a writer.

1506
01:40:23,223 --> 01:40:25,264
W-R-I-T-E-R. One at a time.

1507
01:40:25,344 --> 01:40:25,984
One at a time.

1508
01:40:26,004 --> 01:40:26,984
One at a time.

1509
01:40:27,064 --> 01:40:27,784
One at a time.

1510
01:40:35,071 --> 01:40:44,958
Now, darling, why don't you start?

1511
01:40:45,258 --> 01:40:47,339
Is it true you carried messages from Tomato and Code?

1512
01:40:47,639 --> 01:40:48,260
Of course not.

1513
01:40:48,660 --> 01:40:51,442
I just meet Mr. O'Shaughnessy at Hamburger Heaven and give him the weather report.

1514
01:40:51,942 --> 01:40:54,064
Simply do not ask me what this is all about.

1515
01:40:54,164 --> 01:40:55,525
Pas ce que je ne sais pas, monsieur.

1516
01:40:55,545 --> 01:40:57,247
But you did used to visit Tomato.

1517
01:40:57,527 --> 01:40:58,368
I used to see him every week.

1518
01:40:58,388 --> 01:40:59,028
What's wrong with that?

1519
01:40:59,048 --> 01:41:01,090
Now, you must have known Tomato was part of the narcotics syndicate.

1520
01:41:01,170 --> 01:41:03,052
Mr. Tomato never mentioned narcotics to me.

1521
01:41:03,432 --> 01:41:05,834
Makes me furious the way all these wretched people keep persecuting him.

1522
01:41:06,295 --> 01:41:07,976
He's a deeply sensitive person.

1523
01:41:08,677 --> 01:41:09,858
A darling old man.

1524
01:41:10,118 --> 01:41:10,819
Then you're innocent.

1525
01:41:11,139 --> 01:41:11,920
Of course I'm innocent.

1526
01:41:11,940 --> 01:41:12,860
What are you going to do about it?

1527
01:41:12,981 --> 01:41:13,601
What do you mean?

1528
01:41:13,721 --> 01:41:14,542
Well, who's your lawyer?

1529
01:41:15,883 --> 01:41:16,424
I don't know.

1530
01:41:17,625 --> 01:41:19,046
Mr. O'Shaughnessy, I guess.

1531
01:41:20,342 --> 01:41:21,684
Mr. O'Shaughnessy.

1532
01:41:24,068 --> 01:41:25,831
Grinch, get out of here.

1533
01:41:25,911 --> 01:41:26,732
All right, come on.

1534
01:41:26,792 --> 01:41:27,594
Let's get a move.

1535
01:41:28,014 --> 01:41:28,836
Come on.

1536
01:41:28,856 --> 01:41:29,918
Mr. O'Shaughnessy?

1537
01:41:30,779 --> 01:41:31,720
Oh, shut up.

1538
01:41:32,001 --> 01:41:32,582
Get in there.

1539
01:41:54,966 --> 01:41:55,206
Yeah.

1540
01:41:55,767 --> 01:41:56,967
Mr. Paul Varjack?

1541
01:41:57,107 --> 01:41:57,348
Yeah.

1542
01:41:58,148 --> 01:41:59,929
Ready with Mr. Berman in Hollywood.

1543
01:42:01,170 --> 01:42:04,452
Kindly deposit $3 for the first three minutes, please.

1544
01:42:13,118 --> 01:42:13,419
Hello?

1545
01:42:15,420 --> 01:42:15,740
Hello?

1546
01:42:16,781 --> 01:42:17,762
Old Jay Berman here.

1547
01:42:17,782 --> 01:42:19,042
Who's calling?

1548
01:42:19,663 --> 01:42:21,124
Mr. Berman, this is Paul Varjack.

1549
01:42:22,125 --> 01:42:23,145
Nice to talk to you, kid.

1550
01:42:23,746 --> 01:42:24,226
Varjack.

1551
01:42:24,930 --> 01:42:26,731
V-A-R-J-A-K.

1552
01:42:27,652 --> 01:42:28,672
I'm a friend of Holly's.

1553
01:42:28,892 --> 01:42:30,073
I met you at a party in New York.

1554
01:42:30,373 --> 01:42:30,714
Who?

1555
01:42:31,454 --> 01:42:31,854
Paul.

1556
01:42:32,775 --> 01:42:34,096
Paul Farjack.

1557
01:42:34,736 --> 01:42:39,479
V-A-R... Mr. Berman, this is Fred.

1558
01:42:40,259 --> 01:42:41,560
Oh, Fred baby, huh?

1559
01:42:42,281 --> 01:42:43,541
You call me about the kid, huh?

1560
01:42:45,202 --> 01:42:47,044
Everything's under control, just relax.

1561
01:42:49,425 --> 01:42:50,846
I spoke to my lawyer in New York.

1562
01:42:52,234 --> 01:42:55,576
I told them to take care of everything, send me the bill, but to keep my name anonymous.

1563
01:42:56,077 --> 01:42:56,277
What?

1564
01:42:57,038 --> 01:42:57,458
Unknown.

1565
01:42:57,518 --> 01:42:58,318
I don't want any part of it.

1566
01:42:59,399 --> 01:42:59,860
You hear me?

1567
01:43:00,620 --> 01:43:01,881
You sound like you're in a tunnel.

1568
01:43:02,502 --> 01:43:05,103
Oh, that's this executive phone I have.

1569
01:43:05,644 --> 01:43:05,904
What?

1570
01:43:06,464 --> 01:43:07,405
Executive phone.

1571
01:43:08,526 --> 01:43:10,647
Hey, baby, they only got her in 10,000 bail.

1572
01:43:11,288 --> 01:43:13,209
My lord can get her out till 10 o'clock this morning.

1573
01:43:13,229 --> 01:43:14,010
I'll tell you what you do.

1574
01:43:15,831 --> 01:43:19,554
You bust into that dump she lives in, collect all the junk,

1575
01:43:20,886 --> 01:43:24,368
Get out of the jail, get her out, and take her straight over to a hotel under her phony name, right?

1576
01:43:25,528 --> 01:43:27,609
You want to keep away from the reporters as much as possible.

1577
01:43:28,169 --> 01:43:28,669
You know what I mean?

1578
01:43:28,689 --> 01:43:29,230
Will you do that?

1579
01:43:30,150 --> 01:43:30,890
Sure, Mr. Berman.

1580
01:43:32,111 --> 01:43:33,631
I can't tell you how much I appreciate you.

1581
01:43:33,651 --> 01:43:33,932
Forget it.

1582
01:43:35,992 --> 01:43:36,773
I owe her something.

1583
01:43:38,854 --> 01:43:39,934
Not that I owe her anything.

1584
01:43:39,994 --> 01:43:41,675
I mean, if you really want to get right down to it.

1585
01:43:44,416 --> 01:43:45,377
She's a crazy.

1586
01:43:47,958 --> 01:43:48,698
She's a phony.

1587
01:43:51,082 --> 01:43:52,283
But she's a real phony.

1588
01:43:54,003 --> 01:43:54,744
Know what I mean, kid?

1589
01:43:55,384 --> 01:43:56,304
Yeah, I know what you mean.

1590
01:43:57,885 --> 01:43:58,725
Thanks, Mr. Berman.

1591
01:43:58,985 --> 01:43:59,506
Thanks a lot.

1592
01:44:00,026 --> 01:44:00,286
Right.

1593
01:44:08,589 --> 01:44:09,630
Why don't you behave?

1594
01:44:22,562 --> 01:44:23,743
Hotel night.

1595
01:44:38,055 --> 01:44:39,897
I did a little housebreaking while you were away.

1596
01:44:41,398 --> 01:44:43,399
Clayton Hotel driver, 84th and Madison.

1597
01:44:46,062 --> 01:44:46,342
O.J.

1598
01:44:46,362 --> 01:44:48,404
thinks it'd be a good idea if you stayed out of sight for a while.

1599
01:44:49,104 --> 01:44:50,666
I got most of your stuff here, including Kat.

1600
01:44:51,818 --> 01:44:52,418
He's all right.

1601
01:44:53,539 --> 01:44:53,879
Yeah.

1602
01:44:54,640 --> 01:44:55,360
Hello, cat.

1603
01:44:56,441 --> 01:44:57,542
Poor no-name slob.

1604
01:44:57,562 --> 01:45:02,625
Listen, darling, did you find that plane ticket?

1605
01:45:03,186 --> 01:45:03,546
Right here.

1606
01:45:03,686 --> 01:45:04,346
We can cash it in.

1607
01:45:04,947 --> 01:45:05,487
Cash it in?

1608
01:45:05,527 --> 01:45:06,148
Are you kidding?

1609
01:45:07,008 --> 01:45:07,649
What time is it?

1610
01:45:08,689 --> 01:45:09,670
A little after 10.

1611
01:45:10,110 --> 01:45:10,411
Good.

1612
01:45:11,191 --> 01:45:12,672
Idle Wild Airport, please, driver.

1613
01:45:13,112 --> 01:45:13,553
Never mind.

1614
01:45:14,213 --> 01:45:14,874
You can't do that.

1615
01:45:14,974 --> 01:45:15,274
Why not?

1616
01:45:16,315 --> 01:45:17,175
You don't understand.

1617
01:45:17,215 --> 01:45:18,396
You're under indictment.

1618
01:45:18,516 --> 01:45:21,178
If they catch you jumping, they'll lock you up and throw away the key.

1619
01:45:21,602 --> 01:45:23,043
Don't be ridiculous, darling.

1620
01:45:23,363 --> 01:45:26,265
By the day after tomorrow, I'll be married to the future president of Brazil.

1621
01:45:26,646 --> 01:45:29,007
And that'll give me diplomatic immunity or something.

1622
01:45:31,409 --> 01:45:32,070
I wouldn't bet on it.

1623
01:45:33,951 --> 01:45:34,672
What is it, darling?

1624
01:45:34,692 --> 01:45:39,375
A message for you.

1625
01:45:40,036 --> 01:45:40,136
Oh.

1626
01:45:41,397 --> 01:45:42,077
Oh, yes, I see.

1627
01:45:43,678 --> 01:45:47,341
Did he bring it in person, or was it just there, shoved under the door?

1628
01:45:48,102 --> 01:45:48,582
A cousin.

1629
01:45:52,254 --> 01:45:53,355
Hand me my purse, will you, darling?

1630
01:45:53,375 --> 01:45:57,618
A girl can't read that sort of thing without her lipstick.

1631
01:46:00,680 --> 01:46:01,921
You read to me, will you, darling?

1632
01:46:02,782 --> 01:46:04,723
I don't think I'm quite there.

1633
01:46:05,804 --> 01:46:06,485
Sure you want me to?

1634
01:46:06,825 --> 01:46:07,065
Mm-hmm.

1635
01:46:11,609 --> 01:46:11,729
Okay.

1636
01:46:14,050 --> 01:46:18,594
My dearest little girl, I have loved you knowing you were not as others...

1637
01:46:19,554 --> 01:46:30,399
but conceive of my despair upon discovering in such a brutal and public style how very different you are from the manner of woman a man of my position could hope to make his wife.

1638
01:46:32,960 --> 01:46:42,844
I grieve for the disgrace of your present circumstances, and I do not find it in my heart to add my condemn to the condemn that surrounds you.

1639
01:46:44,885 --> 01:46:47,546
So I hope you will find it in your heart not to condemn me,

1640
01:46:48,938 --> 01:46:55,280
I have my family to protect in my name, and I am a coward where these institutions enter.

1641
01:46:56,940 --> 01:47:00,502
Forget me, beautiful child, and may God be with you.

1642
01:47:01,642 --> 01:47:02,142
Jose.

1643
01:47:05,163 --> 01:47:05,443
Well?

1644
01:47:08,604 --> 01:47:09,684
Well, at least he's honest.

1645
01:47:10,665 --> 01:47:12,085
It's kind of touching.

1646
01:47:12,265 --> 01:47:12,705
Touching?

1647
01:47:12,725 --> 01:47:14,806
That's square ball jazz.

1648
01:47:17,302 --> 01:47:18,463
He says he's a coward.

1649
01:47:18,623 --> 01:47:21,985
All right, so he's not a regular rat or even a super rat.

1650
01:47:22,786 --> 01:47:24,447
He's just a scared little mouse, that's all.

1651
01:47:26,028 --> 01:47:27,389
But oh, golly!

1652
01:47:28,570 --> 01:47:29,810
Gee darn!

1653
01:47:32,933 --> 01:47:35,995
Wow.

1654
01:47:36,935 --> 01:47:37,956
So much for South America.

1655
01:47:40,178 --> 01:47:42,979
I didn't really think you were cut out to be queen of the pampas anyway.

1656
01:47:44,621 --> 01:47:45,321
Clayton Hotel.

1657
01:47:46,322 --> 01:47:46,742
Idlewild.

1658
01:47:47,778 --> 01:47:51,521
The plane leaves at 12, and on it I plan to be.

1659
01:47:52,181 --> 01:47:53,122
Holly, you can't.

1660
01:47:54,463 --> 01:47:55,303
Et pourquoi pas?

1661
01:47:56,604 --> 01:47:58,766
I'm not hot-footing it after Jose, if that's what you think.

1662
01:47:59,006 --> 01:47:59,366
Oh, no.

1663
01:48:00,227 --> 01:48:02,709
As far as I'm concerned, he's the future president of nowhere.

1664
01:48:03,930 --> 01:48:05,871
It's only why should I waste a perfectly good plane ticket?

1665
01:48:06,351 --> 01:48:07,572
Besides, I've never been to Brazil.

1666
01:48:10,634 --> 01:48:12,576
Please, darling, don't sit there looking at me like that.

1667
01:48:14,257 --> 01:48:15,638
I'm going, and that's all there is to it.

1668
01:48:17,302 --> 01:48:21,506
Now, all they want from me or my services is a state's witness against Sally.

1669
01:48:21,886 --> 01:48:23,808
Nobody has any intention of prosecuting me.

1670
01:48:24,689 --> 01:48:26,330
To begin with, they don't have a ghost of a chance.

1671
01:48:28,532 --> 01:48:32,296
Even so, this town's finished for me, at least for a while.

1672
01:48:34,458 --> 01:48:37,600
There are certain shades of limelight that can wreck a girl's complexion.

1673
01:48:38,201 --> 01:48:40,143
They'll have the rope up at every saloon in town.

1674
01:48:42,445 --> 01:48:43,626
I'll tell you what you do for me, darling.

1675
01:48:44,002 --> 01:48:47,523
When you get back to town, I want you to call up the New York Times or whoever you call.

1676
01:48:47,544 --> 01:48:51,405
I want you to mail me a list of the 50 richest men in Brazil.

1677
01:48:51,985 --> 01:48:53,046
The 50 richest!

1678
01:48:55,747 --> 01:48:58,088
Holly, I'm not going to let you do this.

1679
01:48:58,428 --> 01:49:00,049
You're not going to let me.

1680
01:49:00,089 --> 01:49:01,029
Holly, I'm in love with you.

1681
01:49:01,930 --> 01:49:02,410
So what?

1682
01:49:03,050 --> 01:49:03,730
So what?

1683
01:49:05,111 --> 01:49:05,871
So plenty.

1684
01:49:07,072 --> 01:49:07,632
I love you.

1685
01:49:07,672 --> 01:49:08,512
You belong to me.

1686
01:49:09,053 --> 01:49:09,233
No.

1687
01:49:09,953 --> 01:49:11,094
People don't belong to people.

1688
01:49:11,354 --> 01:49:12,114
Of course they do.

1689
01:49:12,386 --> 01:49:14,027
I'm not going to let anyone put me in a cage.

1690
01:49:14,387 --> 01:49:15,607
I don't want to put you in a cage.

1691
01:49:15,647 --> 01:49:16,368
I want to love you.

1692
01:49:16,388 --> 01:49:17,608
It's the same thing.

1693
01:49:18,088 --> 01:49:18,829
No, it's not.

1694
01:49:19,589 --> 01:49:19,969
Holly?

1695
01:49:20,129 --> 01:49:21,050
I'm not Holly.

1696
01:49:21,090 --> 01:49:22,070
I'm not Lula Mae either.

1697
01:49:22,090 --> 01:49:23,151
I don't know who I am.

1698
01:49:23,451 --> 01:49:24,371
I'm like Cat here.

1699
01:49:24,731 --> 01:49:26,092
We're a couple of no-name slobs.

1700
01:49:26,112 --> 01:49:28,113
We belong to nobody, and nobody belongs to us.

1701
01:49:28,133 --> 01:49:29,814
We don't even belong to each other.

1702
01:49:31,574 --> 01:49:32,275
Stop the cab.

1703
01:49:39,058 --> 01:49:39,678
What do you think?

1704
01:49:40,418 --> 01:49:43,120
This ought to be the right kind of place for a tough guy like you.

1705
01:49:43,701 --> 01:49:45,562
Garbage cans, rats galore.

1706
01:49:46,063 --> 01:49:46,663
Scram.

1707
01:49:47,704 --> 01:49:48,525
I said take off.

1708
01:49:49,205 --> 01:49:49,646
Beat it.

1709
01:49:50,066 --> 01:49:50,987
Let's go.

1710
01:49:57,493 --> 01:49:58,213
Driver.

1711
01:49:58,313 --> 01:50:08,362
Pull over here.

1712
01:50:18,018 --> 01:50:20,160
You know what's wrong with you, Miss whoever you are?

1713
01:50:21,581 --> 01:50:22,222
You're chicken.

1714
01:50:22,762 --> 01:50:23,683
You've got no guts.

1715
01:50:24,304 --> 01:50:26,946
You're afraid to stick out your chin and say, OK, life's a fact.

1716
01:50:27,406 --> 01:50:28,507
People do fall in love.

1717
01:50:29,048 --> 01:50:33,472
People do belong to each other, because that's the only chance anybody's got for real happiness.

1718
01:50:34,473 --> 01:50:40,278
You call yourself a free spirit, a wild thing, and you're terrified somebody's going to stick you in a cage.

1719
01:50:41,099 --> 01:50:43,541
Well, baby, you're already in that cage.

1720
01:50:43,561 --> 01:50:44,382
You built it yourself.

1721
01:50:45,066 --> 01:50:48,490
And it's not bounded in the west by Tulip, Texas, or in the east by Somaliland.

1722
01:50:48,530 --> 01:50:49,491
It's wherever you go.

1723
01:50:50,172 --> 01:50:53,255
Because no matter where you run, you just end up running into yourself.

1724
01:51:02,085 --> 01:51:03,627
Here, I've been carrying this thing around for months.

1725
01:51:05,649 --> 01:51:06,289
I don't want it anymore.

